the exception of email, expressing yourself on the Internet wasn't entirely straightforward. Some of us \\\\\\"cool kids\\\\\\" had just left Friendster for MySpace (remember Tom?), but social media hadn't reached critical mass yet, unless you were a millennial listening to emo. Having your own website was probably considered the most professional way to present your digital self.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"59ec\\\\\\">2005-ish Website Builder Toil and Reward</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"82j5p\\\\\\">If your resume listed your own website domain beneath your name, address and cell phone number, when you emailed your resume to jane@staffteamsolutions.com you could practically <b><i>feel</i></b> the distant keystrokes as she wrote back to offer you a temporary, part-time Administration Assistant position at Dodco Insurance - one of Staff Team Solutions <i>premier</i> clients. Not a bad feeling for an under-employed Bachelor of Arts in Communications guy who'd been sulking in the consequences of poor educational life choices. The catch, getting things on the web could be quite the nightmare.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3v6mv\\\\\\">If you were a mere mortal and couldn't afford to pay someone to make a website for you, you'd be left either coding a site in HTML or using Macromedia's Dream Weaver, using the 'Design View' features. The user would then struggle to float various content to the left or right of the page. After chomping at the bit and making yourself crazy with such ditties as &lt;div class=\\\\\\"float-left\\\\\\"&gt; .float-left {float: left;} you'd upload the files via FTP and hope that the slop on the server looked like the slop on your computer. It was awful - sometimes you'd might even wish GeoCities would make a comeback. And it was beautiful.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fgffj\\\\\\">(* a friend of mine just reminded me Live Journal was a thing)</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4befm\\\\\\">Whichever way you chose to get your work done, the sense of accomplishment you received by posting static text to the web was electric. If there\\\\u2019s anything lost from the spirit of the internet in 2005, it\\\\u2019s the DIY ethos\\\\u2014the idea that with just a few lines of code, you could create something truly cool and authentic to yourself. Not unlike a punk band creating a killer song with just a few chords. Ever an optimist, I still believe a young kid in their bedroom can find a way to profoundly change the culture of the internet, but in 2025 all the incentives seem to be directed towards fraternity bros hocking AI.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8k64k\\\\\\">2005, Discovering The Digital Divide</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eili6\\\\\\">2005 was the year the Chicago White Sox baseball team won the world series for the first time in 88 years. I've been a baseball fan ever since, and its left an indelible mark on me. This was also the year my Apple G3 ibook gave up the ghost. It had all my pirated music software like Peak Audio and Native Instrument's Logic Audio. With a salary of $7.00 an hour not only was I without a laptop, but also a key creative outlet. When I moved out of the aforementioned apartment with the communal computer, I also lost my last link to the internet. I had to use the public library to email with friends and apply for jobs. I also started borrowing DVDs and other materials as I couldn't afford to purchase media. This experience helped shape my worldview, and is a key reason I became a librarian. While completing my Master's degree in Library Science I learned this phenomenon I experienced is referred to as the digital divide, and remains a key reason why I believe libraries are so important.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78arq\\\\\\">So What's This Blog Gonna Be About?</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a4l13\\\\\\">On this blog I intend to write about technology, especially especially sharing some of the nerdy things I learn during extensive meditation (ADHD hyper focus) sessions. I have a lot of fun