ock-key=\\\\\\"59ec\\\\\\">2005-ish Website Builder Toil and Reward</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"82j5p\\\\\\">If your resume listed your own website domain beneath your name, address and cell phone number, when you emailed your resume to jane@staffteamsolutions.com you could practically just <b><i>feel</i></b> the distant keystrokes as she wrote back to offer you a temporary, part-time Administration Assistant position at Dodco Insurance - one of Staff Team Solutions <i>premier clients</i>. Not a bad feeling for an under-employed Bachelor of Arts in Communications guy who's been sulking in the consequences of poor educational life choices. The catch, getting things on the web could be quite the nightmare.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3v6mv\\\\\\">If you were a mere mortal like me and couldn't afford to pay someone to make a website for you, you'd be left either coding a site in HTML or using Macromedia's Dream Weaver, using the 'Design View' features. The user would then struggle to float various content to the left or right of the page. After chomping at the bit and making yourself crazy with such ditties as &lt;div class=\\\\\\"float-left\\\\\\"&gt; .float-left {float: left;} you'd upload the files via FTP and hope that the slop on the server looked like the slop on your computer. It was awful, possibly enough to be longing for GeoCities to get resurrected. And it was beautiful.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4befm\\\\\\">Whichever way you chose to get your work done, the sense of accomplishment you received by posting static text to the web was electric. If there\\\\u2019s anything lost from the spirit of the internet in 2005, it\\\\u2019s the DIY ethos\\\\u2014the idea that with just a few lines of code, you could create something truly cool and authentic to yourself. Not unlike a punk band creating a killer song with just a few chords. Ever an optimist, I still believe a young kid in their bedroom will find a way to change the internet for the better, but in 2025, inspired DIY youth seems to be taking a back seat to fraternity bros hocking AI funding by their rich oligarchs parents.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8k64k\\\\\\">2005, Discovering The Digital Divide</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eili6\\\\\\">2005 was the year the Chicago White Sox baseball team won the world series for the first time in 88 years. I've been a baseball fan ever since, and its left an indelible mark on me. This was also the year my Apple G3 ibook gave up the ghost. It had all my pirated music software like Peak Audio and Native Instrument's Logic Audio. With a salary of $7.00 an hour not only was I without a laptop, but also a key creative outlet. When I moved out of the aforementioned apartment with the communal computer, I also lost my last link to the internet. I had to use the public library to email with friends and apply for jobs. I also started borrowing DVDs and other materials, as I couldn't afford to purchase media. This experience helped shape my worldview, and is a key reason I became a librarian. While completing my Master's degree in Library Science I learned this phenomenon is called the digital divide, and remains a key reason to why libraries are such a key ingredient to healthy communities.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78arq\\\\\\">So What's This Blog Gonna Be About?</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a4l13\\\\\\">On this blog I intend to write about technology, especially especially sharing some of the nerdy things I learn during extensive meditation (ADHD hyper focus) sessions. I have a lot of fun messing around with python, JavaScript, and Linux. I also intend to write about project management, because truth is, technology doesn't usually solve problems, <i>great teams do</i>. Scattered here and there will be my various hobbies including cooking, bread making, cycling, video games, music, urban planning, language, vacation and of course, pictures of my cats! While I don't promise much, I'll always make this site ad free, with perhaps a Patreon account.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"4cott\\\\\\">About Me:</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4240t\\\\\\">I grew up in Bayport, New York, a small, mostly flat suburban town sandwiched in between many other small suburban towns for miles in every direction (also known as Long Island). Every important skill, coping mechanism, and deleterious mistake I've made can be traced back to my banal, albeit traumatic existence living there. Despite completing multiple degrees, living in many cities across the US, Canada, and Europe - and having a very rewarding career in IT and libraries, part of me still feels like that lost teenager who learned every Nirvana song on guitar while simultaneously failing math. I currently work for a public library in the interior of British Columbia with my partner Alice, and our three cats Serge, Maisah, and Nala.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"47oqe\\\\\\">Coda</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3ua\\\\\\">If you've stumbled onto this site, I'd like to welcome you to it! I'm not sure how you got here without the magic of Google SEO, but I'm glad you got here anyway - and that's the important thing. Happy New Year!</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6dr5l\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3nn5k\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f5guv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6th\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a28f90e6-1305-4bc1-aab9-3215fefce223\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/youre-blogging-in-2025/", "blog_date": "2025-01-04", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 3, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Remember The Internet of 2005 in 2025", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 36, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 48, "translation_key": "2df69ac9-a9e1-4f59-b3bd-86a5630b411d", "last_published_at": "2025-01-05T03:30:02.771Z", "first_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:47:59.494Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-01-05T04:30:18.062Z"}	\N	6	1	44	1	Remember The Internet of 2005 in 2025
55	2025-01-05 04:52:56.04003+00	{"pk": 6, "live": true, "path": "000100020001", "slug": "youre-blogging-in-2025", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Remember The Internet of 2005 in 2025", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0ly7a\\\\\\">Hello, my name is Dan Lerch and in 2025 I'm choosing to focus on developing my blog. There are two reasons I've made this decision:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aufsq\\\\\\">To escape the limitations of algorithmic and character restricted social media. (It's my blog, I own it, I make the rules, consequences be damned)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c0nv6\\\\\\">To bask in a little internet nostalgia, remembering a time where most aspects of the web weren't entirely polluted by big tech oligarchs.</li></ol><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5ra2f\\\\\\">Personal Reflections of Internet 2005</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3a17t\\\\\\">2005 was an amazing year for the internet. YouTube had just be founded, Web 2.0 was in the works, and Podcasts were just starting to hit their stride. Having just moved to Chicago my new roommate had a desktop PC she graciously let me use whenever I needed it. Her only rule was that I use this obscure browser called Firefox instead of Microsoft's Internet Explorer. Whatever misgivings I may have had quickly gave way to my love for its speed and and innovative user design. Only later did I learn about the open source philosophy of founding company Mozilla. Little did I know at the time that open source would help shape the trajectory of my career.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1akqu\\\\\\">Shortly after discovering Firefox I received an invite to Google's new email platform - something called Gmail? <i>YES - You needed an invite to get it, you couldn't just sign up! </i>It had a full 1 GB of storage and sported a super slick minimalist clean design that felt like years ahead of its competitors. </p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"fo822\\\\\\">Social Media</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fegq0\\\\\\">With the exception of email, expressing yourself on the Internet wasn't entirely straightforward. Some of us \\\\\\"cool kids\\\\\\" had just left Friendster for MySpace, but social media hadn't reached critical mass yet, unless you were a millennial listening to emo. Having your own website was probably considered the most professional way to present your digital self.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"59ec\\\\\\">2005-ish Website Builder Toil and Reward</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"82j5p\\\\\\">If your resume listed your own website domain beneath your name, address and cell phone number, when you emailed your resume to jane@staffteamsolutions.com you could practically just <b><i>feel</i></b> the distant keystrokes as she wrote back to offer you a temporary, part-time Administration Assistant position at Dodco Insurance - one of Staff Team Solutions <i>premier clients</i>. Not a bad feeling for an under-employed Bachelor of Arts in Communications guy who's been sulking in the consequences of poor educational life choices. The catch, getting things on the web could be quite the nightmare.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3v6mv\\\\\\">If you were a mere mortal like me and couldn't afford to pay someone to make a website for you, you'd be left either coding a site in HTML or using Macromedia's Dream Weaver, using the 'Design View' features. The user would then struggle to float various content to the left or right of the page. After chomping at the bit and making yourself crazy with such ditties as &lt;div class=\\\\\\"float-left\\\\\\"&gt; .float-left {float: left;} you'd upload the files via FTP and hope that the slop on the server looked like the slop on your computer. It was awful, possibly enough to be longing for GeoCities to get resurrected. And it was beautiful.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4befm\\\\\\">Whichever way you chose to get your work done, the sense of accomplishment you received by posting static text to the web was electric. If there\\\\u2019s anything lost from the spirit of the internet in 2005, it\\\\u2019s the DIY ethos\\\\u2014the idea that with just a few lines of code, you could create something truly cool and authentic to yourself. Not unlike a punk band creating a killer song with just a few chords. Ever an optimist, I still believe a young kid in their bedroom will find a way to change the internet for the better, but in 2025, inspired DIY youth seems to be taking a back seat to fraternity bros hocking AI funding by their rich oligarchs parents.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8k64k\\\\\\">2005, Discovering The Digital Divide</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eili6\\\\\\">2005 was the year the Chicago White Sox baseball team won the world series for the first time in 88 years. I've been a baseball fan ever since, and its left an indelible mark on me. This was also the year my Apple G3 ibook gave up the ghost. It had all my pirated music software like Peak Audio and Native Instrument's Logic Audio. With a salary of $7.00 an hour not only was I without a laptop, but also a key creative outlet. When I moved out of the aforementioned apartment with the communal computer, I also lost my last link to the internet. I had to use the public library to email with friends and apply for jobs. I also started borrowing DVDs and other materials, as I couldn't afford to purchase media. This experience helped shape my worldview, and is a key reason I became a librarian. While completing my Master's degree in Library Science I learned this phenomenon is called the digital divide, and remains a key reason to why libraries are such a key ingredient to healthy communities.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78arq\\\\\\">So What's This Blog Gonna Be About?</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a4l13\\\\\\">On this blog I intend to write about technology, especially especially sharing some of the nerdy things I learn during extensive meditation (ADHD hyper focus) sessions. I have a lot of fun messing around with python, JavaScript, and Linux. I also intend to write about project management, because truth is, technology doesn't usually solve problems, <i>great teams do</i>. Scattered here and there will be my various hobbies including cooking, bread making, cycling, video games, music, urban planning, language, vacation and of course, pictures of my cats! While I don't promise much, I'll always make this site ad free, with perhaps a Patreon account.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"4cott\\\\\\">About Me</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4240t\\\\\\">I grew up in Bayport, New York, a small, mostly flat suburban town sandwiched in between many other small suburban towns for miles in every direction (also known as Long Island). Every important skill, coping mechanism, and deleterious mistake I've made can be traced back to my banal, albeit traumatic existence living there. Despite completing multiple degrees, living in many cities across the US, Canada, and Europe - and having a very rewarding career in IT and libraries, part of me still feels like that lost teenager who learned every Nirvana song on guitar while simultaneously failing math. I currently work for a public library in the interior of British Columbia with my partner Alice, and our three cats Serge, Maisah, and Nala.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"47oqe\\\\\\">Coda</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3ua\\\\\\">If you've stumbled onto this site, I'd like to welcome you to it! I'm not sure how you got here without the magic of Google SEO, but I'm glad you got here anyway - and that's the important thing. Happy New Year!</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6dr5l\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3nn5k\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f5guv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6th\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a28f90e6-1305-4bc1-aab9-3215fefce223\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/youre-blogging-in-2025/", "blog_date": "2025-01-04", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 3, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Remember The Internet of 2005 in 2025", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 36, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 54, "translation_key": "2df69ac9-a9e1-4f59-b3bd-86a5630b411d", "last_published_at": "2025-01-05T03:30:02.771Z", "first_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:47:59.494Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-01-05T04:49:13.483Z"}	\N	6	1	44	1	Remember The Internet of 2005 in 2025
9	2025-01-03 01:04:37.544085+00	{"pk": 6, "live": true, "path": "000100020001", "slug": "youre-blogging-in-2025", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "You're blogging in 2025?", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0ly7a\\\\\\">In the decaying age of social media fueled by micro-blogging, why am I blogging?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"58c7e\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"20a1j\\\\\\">In a world dominated by microblogging platforms and social media, maintaining a personal blog may seem quaint, even archaic. Platforms like X, Instagram, and TikTok provide instant gratification, a massive audience, and effortless sharing. Yet, I find myself gravitating toward my personal blog, a space that\\\\u2019s entirely my own. Here\\\\u2019s why I\\\\u2019ve chosen this path and why I think others might find value in it too.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"9fmma\\\\\\">Ownership and Control</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7hhhn\\\\\\">One of the main reasons I maintain a personal blog is the sense of ownership it provides. Social media platforms are subject to change\\\\u2014algorithms shift, policies evolve, and sometimes entire platforms vanish. When you blog on your own domain, you have full control over your content, its presentation, and its permanence. You\\\\u2019re not at the mercy of a third party deciding what\\\\u2019s visible or important.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7u4a1\\\\\\">Depth and Thoughtfulness</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6f4o7\\\\\\">Microblogging encourages brevity, often sacrificing nuance in favor of punchy soundbites. While this format has its merits, it doesn\\\\u2019t allow for the same level of depth and thoughtfulness as a long-form blog post. On my blog, I can explore topics comprehensively, crafting arguments and sharing experiences without character limits. Writing becomes a reflective process rather than a race for fleeting attention.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88c6m\\\\\\">Authenticity and Privacy</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"32v9h\\\\\\">Social media often demands a polished, curated version of ourselves. The quest for likes, shares, and followers can create a performative dynamic that feels disconnected from authenticity. My blog is a space where I can be true to myself without the pressure of performance metrics. I can write for an audience of one or a hundred without tailoring my content to fit a trending hashtag.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"ediv0\\\\\\">A Timeless Archive</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ftohg\\\\\\">A personal blog serves as an evolving archive of my thoughts, experiences, and growth over time. It\\\\u2019s more than a stream of ephemeral updates; it\\\\u2019s a repository I can revisit and reflect upon. Unlike social media, where older posts often fade into obscurity, a blog allows for easy retrieval and re-engagement with past content.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"agf40\\\\\\">Fostering Meaningful Connections</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"jaai\\\\\\">While social media fosters quantity over quality in interactions, blogging tends to attract readers who genuinely resonate with your thoughts. The comments section of a blog often becomes a space for deeper discussions, unhurried by the frenetic pace of a social media feed. These meaningful connections feel more enduring and valuable to me.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"dvtfl\\\\\\">Learning and Skill Development</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5u3hd\\\\\\">Blogging isn\\\\u2019t just about sharing ideas; it\\\\u2019s also about honing skills. Writing, editing, design, and even basic web development are integral to maintaining a personal blog. These skills not only enhance my blog but also contribute to my personal and professional growth. Social media, by contrast, often feels like a passive experience rather than an active, creative one.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cnolr\\\\\\">Digital Serenity</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7206d\\\\\\">Finally, blogging provides a sense of digital serenity. Unlike the chaos of social media, with its endless notifications and algorithm-driven content, my blog is a calm space where I can engage with the digital world on my terms. It\\\\u2019s a respite from the noise, a place where quality triumphs over quantity.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"a9ug3\\\\\\">Conclusion</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7ai0o\\\\\\">While microblogging and social media certainly have their place, maintaining a personal blog offers a different kind of fulfillment. It\\\\u2019s a practice rooted in ownership, depth, authenticity, and meaningful connection. In a fast-paced, fleeting digital age, my blog remains a sanctuary\\\\u2014a place to slow down, reflect, and truly be myself.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a28f90e6-1305-4bc1-aab9-3215fefce223\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/youre-blogging-in-2025/", "blog_date": "2025-01-02", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 3, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "You're blogging in 2025?", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 7, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 7, "translation_key": "2df69ac9-a9e1-4f59-b3bd-86a5630b411d", "last_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:47:59.494Z", "first_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:47:59.494Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2024-12-28T00:47:59.477Z"}	\N	6	1	44	1	You're blogging in 2025?
