hyper focus) sessions. I have a lot of fun messing around with python, JavaScript, and Linux. I also intend to write about project management, because truth is, technology doesn't usually solve problems, <i>great teams do</i>. Scattered here and there will be my various hobbies including cooking, bread making, cycling, video games, music, urban planning, language, vacation and of course, pictures of my cats! While I don't promise much, I'll always make this site ad free, with perhaps a Patreon account.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4cott\\\\\\"><b>About Me:</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4240t\\\\\\">I grew up in Bayport, New York, a small, mostly flat suburban town sandwiched in between many other small suburban towns for miles in every direction (also known as Long Island). Every important skill, coping mechanism, and deleterious mistake I've made can be traced back to my banal, albeit traumatic existence living there. Despite completing multiple degrees, living in many cities across the US, Canada, and Europe - and having a very rewarding career in IT and libraries, part of me still feels like that lost teenager who learned every Nirvana song on guitar while simultaneously failing math. I currently work for a public library in the interior of British Columbia with my partner Alice, and our three cats Serge, Maisah, and Nala.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"47oqe\\\\\\">If you've stumbled onto this site, I'd like to welcome you to it! I'm not sure how you got here without the magic of Google SEO, but I'm glad you got here anyway - and that's the important thing. Happy New Year!</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6dr5l\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3nn5k\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f5guv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6th\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a28f90e6-1305-4bc1-aab9-3215fefce223\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/youre-blogging-in-2025/", "blog_date": "2025-01-02", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 3, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "It's 2025, Blog like it's 2025!", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 22, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 34, "translation_key": "2df69ac9-a9e1-4f59-b3bd-86a5630b411d", "last_published_at": "2025-01-05T01:33:55.156Z", "first_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:47:59.494Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-01-05T03:12:19.941Z"}	\N	6	1	44	1	It's 2025, Blog like it's 2025!
105	2025-01-05 20:37:11.60836+00	{"pk": 6, "live": true, "path": "000100020001", "slug": "internet-2005", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Remembering The Internet of 2005 in 2025", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0ly7a\\\\\\">Hello, my name is Dan Lerch and in 2025 I'm choosing to maintain an independent blog. There are two reasons I'm doing this:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aufsq\\\\\\">To escape the limitations of algorithmic and character restricted social media. It's my independently created blog, I own it, and I make the rules.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c0nv6\\\\\\">To bask in a little internet nostalgia, remembering a time where most aspects of the web weren't entirely polluted by big tech oligarchs.</li></ol><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5ra2f\\\\\\">Personal Reflections of Internet 2005</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3a17t\\\\\\">2005 was an amazing year for the internet. YouTube had just been founded, Web 2.0 was in the works, and podcasts were just starting to hit their stride. Having just moved to Chicago my new roommate had a desktop PC she graciously let me use whenever I needed it. Her only rule was that I use this obscure browser called Firefox instead of Microsoft's Internet Explorer. Whatever misgivings I may have had quickly gave way to my love for its speed and innovative user design. Only later did I learn about the open source philosophy of founding company Mozilla. A few years later the open source ecosystem would help shape the trajectory of my career.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1akqu\\\\\\">Shortly after discovering Firefox I received an invite to Google's new email platform - something called Gmail? <i>YES - You needed an invite, you couldn't just sign up!</i> It had a full 1 GB of storage and sported a super slick, minimalist design that felt years ahead of its competitors.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"fo822\\\\\\">Social Media</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fegq0\\\\\\">With the exception of email, expressing yourself on the Internet wasn't entirely straightforward. Some of us \\\\\\"cool kids\\\\\\" had just left Friendster for MySpace (remember Tom?), but social media hadn't reached critical mass yet, unless you were a millennial listening to emo. Having your own website was probably considered the most professional way to present your digital self.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"59ec\\\\\\">2005-ish Website Builder Toil and Reward</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"82j5p\\\\\\">Being young and living in a big city often meant finding work wherever you could. Staffing agencies were often your best way to get your foot in the door. I had this idea that if my resume included a home-made professional website, that when I emailed my resume to jane@staffteamsolutions.com, shortly thereafter I'd practically <b><i>feel</i></b> the distant keystrokes as she gleefully wrote back to offer me a temporary, part-time Administration Assistant position at Dodco Insurance (one of Staff Team Solutions <i>premier</i> clients!). Not a bad feeling for an under-employed Bachelor of Arts in Communications graduate who'd been sulking in the consequences of poor educational life choices. The catch, making a website was often a nightmare.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3v6mv\\\\\\">If you were a mere mortal and couldn't afford to pay someone to make a website for you, you'd be left either coding a site in HTML or using Macromedia's Dream Weaver, using the 'Design View' features. The user would then struggle to float various content to the left or right of the page. After chomping at the bit and making yourself crazy with such ditties as:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"5gcqd\\\\\\">&lt;div style=\\\\\\"float: right;\\\\\\"&gt;</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev9pi\\\\\\">&lt;tr&gt; &lt;td colspan=\\\\\\"2\\\\\\"&gt;Hobbies&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan=\\\\\\"2\\\\\\"&gt;Experience&lt;/tr&gt;</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8clil\\\\\\">After your masterpiece was created, you'd upload the files via FTP and hope that the slop on the server looked like the slop on your computer. Sometimes a simple edit would go horribly wrong and you'd find the grid you spent hours trying to get right looked more like spaghetti thrown at the wall than a solid HTML foundation. It was awful - enough to even wish GeoCities would return. It was also simultaneously beautiful.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4befm\\\\\\">Whichever way you chose to get your work done, the sense of accomplishment you received by posting static text to the web was electric. If there\\\\u2019s anything lost from the spirit of the internet in 2005, it\\\\u2019s the DIY ethos\\\\u2014the idea that with just a few lines of code, you could create something truly cool and authentic to yourself. Not unlike a punk band creating a killer song with just a few chords. Ever an optimist, I still believe a young kid in their bedroom can find a way to profoundly change the culture of the internet, but in 2025 all the incentives seem to be directed towards fraternity bros hocking AI.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1714r\\\\\\">(* a friend of mine just reminded me Live Journal was a thing, and technically still is)</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8k64k\\\\\\">2005, Experiencing The Digital Divide</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eili6\\\\\\">2005 was the year the Chicago White Sox baseball team won the world series for the first time in 88 years. I've been a baseball fan ever since, and its left an indelible mark on me. This was also the year my Apple G3 ibook gave up the ghost. It had all my pirated music software like Peak Audio and Native Instrument's Logic Audio. With a salary of $7.00 an hour not only was I without a laptop, but also a key creative outlet. When I moved out of the aforementioned apartment with the communal computer, I also lost my last link to the internet. I had to use the public library to email with friends and apply for jobs. I also started borrowing DVDs and other materials as I couldn't afford to purchase media. This experience helped shape my worldview, and is a key reason I became a librarian. While completing my Master's degree in Library Science I learned this phenomenon I was experiencing is called the digital divide, and remains a key reason why I believe libraries are so important.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78arq\\\\\\">So What's This Blog Gonna Be About?</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a4l13\\\\\\">On this blog I intend to write about technology, especially especially sharing some of the nerdy things I learn during extensive meditation (ADHD hyper focus) sessions. I have a lot of fun messing around with python, JavaScript, and Linux. I also intend to write about project management, because the truth is technology doesn't usually solve problems, <i>great teams do</i>. Scattered here and there I will post about my various hobbies including cooking, bread making, cycling, video games, music, urban planning, language, vacation and of course ... many pictures of my cats! While I don't promise much, I'll always make this site ad free, with perhaps a Patreon account.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"4cott\\\\\\">About Me</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4240t\\\\\\">I grew up in Bayport, New York, a small, mostly flat suburban town sandwiched in between many other small suburban towns for miles in every direction (also known as Long Island). Every important skill, coping mechanism, and deleterious mistake I've made can be traced back to my mostly banal, but sometimes traumatic existence. Despite completing multiple degrees, living in many cities across the US, Canada, and Europe - and having a very rewarding career in IT and libraries, part of me still feels like that lost teenager who learned every Nirvana song on guitar while simultaneously flunking math. I currently work for a public library in the interior of British Columbia and live with my partner Alice. We have three lovely cats, Serge, Maisah, and Nala.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqn0m\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8d097\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"6\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Young Dan Lerch on a bike\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brfc7\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"47oqe\\\\\\">Coda</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3ua\\\\\\">If you've stumbled onto this site, I'd like to welcome you! I'm not sure how you got here without the magic of Google SEO, but I'm glad you did. Happy New Year!</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6th\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a28f90e6-1305-4bc1-aab9-3215fefce223\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/internet-2005/", "blog_date": "2025-01-04", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 4, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Remembering The Internet of 2005 in 2025", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 103, "show_in_menus": true, "latest_revision": 103, "translation_key": "2df69ac9-a9e1-4f59-b3bd-86a5630b411d", "last_published_at": "2025-01-05T20:28:37.460Z", "first_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:47:59.494Z", "search_description": "Remembering the internet of 2005, and a few snippets of life in Chicago", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-01-05T20:28:37.439Z"}	\N	6	1	44	1	Remembering The Internet of 2005 in 2025
148	2025-06-20 03:44:14.978113+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bu0hm\\\\\\"><b>Step 4:</b> Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">create the gnome-kiosk-script for your user by entering in a terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"igkce\\\\\\">Install pyqt5 and test launch the kiosk browser</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6n6eq\\\\\\">I've developed this kiosk browser using pyqt5. With PyQt5 you can easily build your own chromium based browser experience with all the bells and whistles you need. For the purposes of this tutorial I'll show you how to download and install my project. The intricacies of coding in Python are out of scope for this tutorial. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cdlro\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"br1iv\\\\\\">Install python3-pip, which is used to install external python dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pip</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"8l3s7\\\\\\">Install git with sudo apt install git</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"a63lr\\\\\\">Install dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pyqt5 python3-pyqt5.qtwebengine</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"66cdi\\\\\\">Download the kiosk browser code from gitlab: git clone https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"5gp0s\\\\\\">Open the file kiosk-webpage.txt. Replace the duckduckgo search engine with the webpage of your choice.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3je9i\\\\\\">Launch the browser: python kiosk-browser.py. When you're finished you can hit Ctrl + c to exit the terminal process.</li></ol>\\", \\"id\\": \\"78f6be1f-c6c1-4a09-8554-6910c18837d2\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"eh3tl\\\\\\">Auto Launch the Browser in Kiosk Mode</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9e3d3\\\\\\">We'll want the kiosk browser to load on boot. To do this, we need to edit gnome-kiosk configuration file we created earlier. In the terminal enter the following command:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"7d03e951-3a7c-4f01-9da6-cd90f9f8acde\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"nano /.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"752161b5-2183-4d66-9fe4-28fb2bea854b\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"o8ojl\\\\\\">put a # in front of the line gnome-editor ~/.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script (or just delete it). </p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"49a1681f-8759-4f04-8bb4-5a9f9f056f41\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 147, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-20T03:32:40.405Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
