41	2025-01-05 03:57:21.255182+00	{"pk": 6, "live": true, "path": "000100020001", "slug": "youre-blogging-in-2025", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Remember The Internet of 2005 in 2025", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0ly7a\\\\\\">Hello, my name is Dan Lerch and in 2025 I'm choosing to focus on my blog. There are two main reasons I've made this decision:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aufsq\\\\\\">To escape the limitations of algorithmic and character restricted social media. (It's my blog, I own it, I make the rules, consequences be damned)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c0nv6\\\\\\">To bask in a little internet nostalgia, remembering a time where most aspects of the web weren't entirely polluted by big tech oligarchs.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5ra2f\\\\\\"><b>Thinking Back to 2005</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3a17t\\\\\\">2005 was an amazing year for the internet. YouTube had just be founded, Web 2.0 was in the works, and Podcasts were just starting to hit their stride. Having just moved to Chicago my new roommate had a desktop PC she graciously let me use. Her only rule was that I use Firefox instead of Internet Explorer, which until that time I had never heard of. Little did I know this world of open source would shape the trajectory of my career, but I'm planning on writing about this in a future post. I also got an invite to Google's new email platform - something called Gmail? It had a full 1 GB of storage and sported a super slick minimalist clean design that felt like years ahead of its competitors. With the exception of email, expressing yourself on the Internet wasn't entirely straightforward. Some of us \\\\\\"cool kids\\\\\\" had just left Friendster for MySpace, but social media hadn't reached critical mass yet. Having your own website was probably considered the most professional way to present your digital self.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"59ec\\\\\\"><b>2005-ish Website Builder Toil and Reward</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"82j5p\\\\\\">If your resume listed your own website domain beneath your name, address and cell phone number, when you emailed your resume to jane@staffteamsolutions.com you could practically just <b><i>feel</i></b> the distant keystrokes as she wrote back to offer you a temporary, part-time Administration Assistant position at Dodco Insurance - one of Staff Team Solutions <i>premier clients</i>. Not a bad feeling for an under-employed Bachelor of Arts in Communications guy who's been sulking in the consequences of poor educational life choices. The catch, getting things on the web could be quite the nightmare.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3v6mv\\\\\\">If you were a mere mortal like me and couldn't afford to pay someone to make a website for you, you'd be left either coding a site in HTML or using Macromedia's Dream Weaver, using the 'Design View' features. The user would then struggle to float various content to the left or right of the page. After chomping at the bit and making yourself crazy with such ditties as &lt;div class=\\\\\\"float-left\\\\\\"&gt; .float-left {float: left;} you'd upload the files via FTP and hope that the slop on the server looked like the slop on your computer. It was awful. And it was beautiful. Whichever way you chose to get your work done, the sense of accomplishment you got by posting static text to the web was electric. If there's anything I miss from the spirit of internet 2025 , was a DIY sense that if one could patch together a few lines of code, you might be able to build something really cool and authentic to you. Not unlike a punk band creating a killer song with only a few chords. Don't get me wrong, a young kid in their garage can still change the world, but in 2025 DIY seems to be taking a back seat to tech oligarchs hocking AI.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eili6\\\\\\">2005 was also the year the Chicago White Sox baseball team won the world series for the first time in 88 years. It was also the year my laptop, an Apple G3 ibook gave up the ghost. It had all my pirated music software on it like Peak and Logic Audio. Only earning $7.00 an hour while trying to pay off $3000 of credit debt and rent meant left me at the wrong side of the digital divide, so maybe it wasn't a total renaissance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a4l13\\\\\\">On this blog I intend to write about technology, especially especially sharing some of the nerdy things I learn during extensive meditation (ADHD hyper focus) sessions. I have a lot of fun messing around with python, JavaScript, and Linux. I also intend to write about project management, because truth is, technology doesn't usually solve problems, <i>great teams do</i>. Scattered here and there will be my various hobbies including cooking, bread making, cycling, video games, music, urban planning, language, vacation and of course, pictures of my cats! While I don't promise much, I'll always make this site ad free, with perhaps a Patreon account.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4cott\\\\\\"><b>About Me:</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4240t\\\\\\">I grew up in Bayport, New York, a small, mostly flat suburban town sandwiched in between many other small suburban towns for miles in every direction (also known as Long Island). Every important skill, coping mechanism, and deleterious mistake I've made can be traced back to my banal, albeit traumatic existence living there. Despite completing multiple degrees, living in many cities across the US, Canada, and Europe - and having a very rewarding career in IT and libraries, part of me still feels like that lost teenager who learned every Nirvana song on guitar while simultaneously failing math. I currently work for a public library in the interior of British Columbia with my partner Alice, and our three cats Serge, Maisah, and Nala.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"47oqe\\\\\\">If you've stumbled onto this site, I'd like to welcome you to it! I'm not sure how you got here without the magic of Google SEO, but I'm glad you got here anyway - and that's the important thing. Happy New Year!</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6dr5l\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3nn5k\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f5guv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6th\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a28f90e6-1305-4bc1-aab9-3215fefce223\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/youre-blogging-in-2025/", "blog_date": "2025-01-02", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 3, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Remember The Internet of 2005 in 2025", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 36, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 40, "translation_key": "2df69ac9-a9e1-4f59-b3bd-86a5630b411d", "last_published_at": "2025-01-05T03:30:02.771Z", "first_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:47:59.494Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-01-05T03:57:08.248Z"}	\N	6	1	44	1	Remember The Internet of 2005 in 2025
7	2024-12-28 00:47:59.477211+00	{"pk": 6, "live": true, "path": "000100020001", "slug": "youre-blogging-in-2025", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "You're blogging in 2025?", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0ly7a\\\\\\">In the decaying age of social media fueled by micro-blogging, why am I blogging?</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a28f90e6-1305-4bc1-aab9-3215fefce223\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome-to-my-website/youre-blogging-in-2025/", "blog_date": "2025-01-02", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 3, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "You're blogging in 2025?", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": null, "translation_key": "2df69ac9-a9e1-4f59-b3bd-86a5630b411d", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": null}	\N	6	1	44	1	You're blogging in 2025?
