ta-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bu0hm\\\\\\"><b>Step 4:</b> Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">create the gnome-kiosk-script for your user by entering in a terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"igkce\\\\\\">Install pyqt5 and test launch the kiosk browser</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6n6eq\\\\\\">I've developed this kiosk browser using pyqt5. With PyQt5 you can easily build your own chromium based browser experience with all the bells and whistles you need. For the purposes of this tutorial I'll show you how to download and install my project. The intricacies of coding in Python are out of scope for this tutorial.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cdlro\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"br1iv\\\\\\">Install python3-pip, which is used to install external python dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pip</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"8l3s7\\\\\\">Install git with sudo apt install git</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"a63lr\\\\\\">Install dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pyqt5 python3-pyqt5.qtwebengine</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"66cdi\\\\\\">Download the kiosk browser code from gitlab: git clone https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"5gp0s\\\\\\">Open the file kiosk-webpage.txt. Replace the duckduckgo search engine with the webpage of your choice.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3je9i\\\\\\">Launch the browser: python kiosk-browser.py. When you're finished you can hit Ctrl + c to exit the terminal process.</li></ol>\\", \\"id\\": \\"78f6be1f-c6c1-4a09-8554-6910c18837d2\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"eh3tl\\\\\\">out of kiosk mode you can enter Ctrl + Alt + F3Auto Launch the Browser in Kiosk Mode</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9e3d3\\\\\\">We'll want the kiosk browser to load on boot. To do this, we need to edit gnome-kiosk configuration file we created earlier. In the terminal enter the following command:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"7d03e951-3a7c-4f01-9da6-cd90f9f8acde\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"nano /.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"752161b5-2183-4d66-9fe4-28fb2bea854b\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"o8ojl\\\\\\">put a # in front of the line gnome-editor ~/.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script (or just delete it). Then add the following lines:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e6t5m\\\\\\">cd ~/kiosk-browser</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"58sht\\\\\\">python3 kiosk-browser.py</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6k4vs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bi9ig\\\\\\">If you've done it correctly, it will look like the image below:</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"8\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"How your gnome-kiosk-script file should look after editing\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bhmi6\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1hvrc\\\\\\">Getting Into Kiosk Mode:</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7ieu4\\\\\\">To start gnome kiosk, log out. Now at the login screen, select the gear icon on the lower right. Select Gnome Kiosk Script (Wayland Display Server). Login with your username and password and the browser should load.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"95c1a\\\\\\">Leaving Kiosk Mode</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8hu30\\\\\\">To get out of kiosk mode enter Ctrl + Alt + F3. You'll be launched into a full screen terminal. Login with your username and password. Type reboot and hit enter. After your PC reboots you can switch the display back to Ubuntu (Gnome).</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"55caj\\\\\\">Recommended Settings</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u7rr\\\\\\">On the kiosk account you'll want to set a few things before launching to your users. In settings, adjust the power settings to your liking, and set blank screen to never. Open the software and Updates app and adjust the update schedule to your liking. I'd recommend setting the updates to install automatically.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1m0s\\\\\\">Securing Accounts and Automatic Login</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cji60\\\\\\">Let's remove the administrative rights from our kiosk user. To do this, log out of the kiosk account, and login in with the administrator. In Settings --&gt; system users, toggle off Administrator. Toggle on Automatic Login. </p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"f8mv0\\\\\\">Last Steps!</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bbo2r\\\\\\">1) Log out of the Administrator Account</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9gtr3\\\\\\">2) At the Login Screen, select the kiosk user, and re-enable Gnome Kiosk (Wayland Server). </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"baodm\\\\\\">3) Log in with your username and password and you're good to go!</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e93uc\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"49a1681f-8759-4f04-8bb4-5a9f9f056f41\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 161, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-20T04:51:41.130Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
208	2025-06-25 06:31:36.841753+00	{"pk": 8, "live": true, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk at our library. Porteus Kiosk, a modified Linux operating system, provides a limited web browser experience, often used to allow clientele to browse the inventory of a store, or in my case, the library's collection. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and older versions had been completely scrubbed from their website. Rather than sign us up for yet another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and PyQt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">My goals were simple:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser, limited to just a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">There needed to be accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer need to be in place that would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ckl7o\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"e56sd\\\\\\">The Browser:</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4553q\\\\\\">After toiling with numerous chrome and Firefox extensions, as well as Microsoft Window's Kiosk mode, I came to the sinking realization that I was going to have to develop my own in-house solution. Fortunately, with a little python skills (and a lot of chatGPT), I was able to develop my own Kiosk browser using <a href=\\\\\\"https://pypi.org/project/PyQt5/\\\\\\">PyQt5</a>. I won't bore you with the details, but suffice to say you can download my work, or dig into my code over at <a href=\\\\\\"https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser\\\\\\">gitlab</a>. The browser itself should work on most versions of Linux, including some later models of the raspberry pi, but also Windows and Mac.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dduf1\\\\\\">Note, this app won't stop users from closing the browser and starting other applications, this is where Gnome Kiosk comes into play, and why this tutorial focuses only on Linux.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"57f8n\\\\\\">The Kiosk Environment</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9evt7\\\\\\">I used Gnome Kiosk to deploy a kiosk experience. Per their <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Github repository</a>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bo3o\\\\\\">\\\\\\"GNOME Kiosk provides a desktop environment suitable for fixed purpose, or single application deployments like wall displays and point-of-sale systems.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ej0q8\\\\\\">It provides a very minimal wayland display server and compositor and Xorg compositor and window manager. It automatically starts applications fullscreen.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9t5c\\\\\\">Notably, GNOME Kiosk features no panels, dashes, or docks that could distract from the application using it as a platform.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8v5nn\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available to install on many Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7u6e0\\\\\\"><b>Knowledge Assumptions:</b> You should be comfortable installing Linux based systems and be somewhat familiar with the terminal.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"52g13\\\\\\">Instructions:</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n8f3\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop. While going through the install prompts create a user called kiosk.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d216h\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu. You can also do this in the terminal\\\\\\"</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"1cc800ec-9e78-4570-b82e-4367add3f6c1\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create an additional user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we've fully configured the kiosk and removed the admin rights for the kiosk user.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"595j4\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"9\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"create Administrator user\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8o1ff\\\\\\"><b>Step 4:</b> Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"v85xg\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"10\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Terminal windows showing installing gnome kiosk\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e1j3q\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"5a679b2e-a6f1-4fb4-b14c-87cc0afdab14\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">We'll start off by creating a gnome kiosk script that we'll edit later to auto start the browser. Enter the following in the terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"11\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"generating a gnome kiosk script file\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"145a3\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear on screen. This file is located in ~/.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script. (or /home/kiosk/.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script). You can close the file by typing ctrl + c. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"igkce\\\\\\">Installing PyQt5 and Downloading the Kiosk Code From Gitlab</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6n6eq\\\\\\">The code snippet below will install python3 pip, git, and pyqt5, including the web engine, the necessary dependencies to run the kiosk browser.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"78f6be1f-c6c1-4a09-8554-6910c18837d2\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install python3-pip && sudo apt install git && sudo apt install python3-pyqt5 && sudo apt install python3-pyqt5.qtwebengine\\", \\"id\\": \\"84d81283-a4a9-48c7-9237-6c5722d10c52\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"p9eod\\\\\\">Download the kiosk browser code from gitlab:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a32c3dfd-d94c-45dd-b399-7ed4c5d52e59\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"git clone https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser\\", \\"id\\": \\"ab8a44d4-a57d-4d1e-bba7-d78997f1c4cf\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"p9eod\\\\\\">Inside the folder you'll see a file called kiosk-webpage.txt. Replace the duckduckgo.com search engine with the webpage of your choice (or just leave it at duckduckgo.com, your choice).</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"775194be-ab9b-4ee9-b803-d19a7555a79c\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"cd kiosk-browser\\", \\"id\\": \\"ed1b008d-f3d6-4853-9a09-c77fec026e1b\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"python kiosk-browser.py\\", \\"id\\": \\"0e270e8d-4ddd-481a-b09d-97326b11bceb\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4myqm\\\\\\">When you're finished you can hit Ctrl + c to exit</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8nht4\\\\\\"><b>Note (TLDR):</b> python programmers will tell you that you should run your projects in isolated python virtual environments. While I agree with them, I'd preferring to keep this tutorial as simple as I can make it, and truth be told, your kiosk should run absolutely fine without completing these steps. If you're really curious, you can check out the <a href=\\\\\\"https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser\\\\\\">gitlab page</a> where I describe getting how to run the kiosk in a python virtual environment. I should also mention that as of this writing PyQt5 will not install properly on a raspberry pi using the virtual environment with the pip package.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"ffce5cee-94c5-49bf-a394-4487c26e7e9c\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"eh3tl\\\\\\">Setting the Kiosk to Load on Boot</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1a2sk\\\\\\">When the gnome kiosk desktop environment is launches, we'll need the kiosk browser to load. This is done by editing the gnome-kiosk configuration file we created earlier. In the terminal enter the following command:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"7d03e951-3a7c-4f01-9da6-cd90f9f8acde\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"nano /.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"752161b5-2183-4d66-9fe4-28fb2bea854b\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"o8ojl\\\\\\">put a # in front of the line gnome-editor ~/.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script (or just delete it). Then add the following lines:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e6t5m\\\\\\">cd ~/kiosk-browser</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"58sht\\\\\\">python3 kiosk-browser.py</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6k4vs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bi9ig\\\\\\">If you've done it correctly, it will look like the image below:</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"8\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"How your gnome-kiosk-script file should look after editing\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bhmi6\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1hvrc\\\\\\">Getting Into Kiosk Mode:</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7ieu4\\\\\\">To start gnome kiosk, log out of your session. Now at the login screen, select the gear icon on the lower right. Select Gnome Kiosk Script (Wayland Display Server). Login with your username and password and the browser should load. (side note, X11 is quickly reaching end-of-life and should be avoided whenever possible)</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cvk3d\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"12\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"kiosk script session (wayland display server)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7q3f9\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"95c1a\\\\\\"></h2>\\", \\"id\\": \\"49a1681f-8759-4f04-8bb4-5a9f9f056f41\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fn96t\\\\\\">If you've set everything up correctly, the kiosk should be running!</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"13\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Gnome Kiosk &amp; the kiosk browser running\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c396\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"f4b6u\\\\\\">Exiting The Kiosk Environment</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8hu30\\\\\\">To get out of kiosk mode enter Ctrl + Alt + F3. You'll be launched into a full screen terminal. Login with your username and password. Type reboot and hit enter. After your PC reboots you can switch the display back to Ubuntu, or log in as the Administrator account. Admittedly, it's annoying to have to reboot, and I haven't figured out an alternative.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"55caj\\\\\\">Recommended Settings</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u7rr\\\\\\">On the kiosk account you'll want to set a few things before launching to your users. In settings, adjust the power settings to your liking, and set blank screen to never. Open the software and Updates app and adjust the update schedule to your liking. I'd recommend setting the updates to install automatically.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1m0s\\\\\\">Securing Accounts and Automatic Login</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cji60\\\\\\">Let's remove the administrative rights from our kiosk user. To do this, log out of the kiosk account, and login in with the administrator. In Settings --&gt; system users. On the kiosk user, toggle off administrator. We'll also toggle on automatic login, so our kiosks loads at boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"da9q8\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"14\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Auto login and remove admin rights.\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ddl92\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"f8mv0\\\\\\">Last Steps</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bbo2r\\\\\\">1) Log out of the Administrator Account</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9gtr3\\\\\\">2) At the Login Screen, select the kiosk user, and re-enable Gnome Kiosk (Wayland Server).</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"baodm\\\\\\">3) Log in with your username and password and you're good to go!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5lmc7\\\\\\">Turning Off Kiosk Mode after Auto Logon has been enabled</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cc75s\\\\\\">If you've made a mistake, or wish to make any changes, you'll need to re-enable the default desktop environment by using the command line. Hit Ctrl + alt + F3 to load the terminal. Then enter:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"h1qh\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b38c812-3df1-43df-9766-99d7ccd44edf\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"su Administrator\\", \\"id\\": \\"a55b5e72-9866-4c1d-9e6f-595e6e53346c\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kyxtn\\\\\\">(enter the admin password)</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b3f1bad-0b62-40c7-978d-0ccc8dccf884\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo nano /var/lib/AccountsService/users/kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"9f7ac349-ce1c-45f3-8642-547eba9bd872\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5i18s\\\\\\">Enter the following command to edit the default session of the kiosk account:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2qbjd\\\\\\"><i>put a # in front of the Session=gnome-kiosk-script-wayland to comment it out. Write the following</i></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"3cd49aca-8b6e-4cba-9812-d519a6ebb4c4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"Session=Ubuntu\\", \\"id\\": \\"8334d44d-34a6-4b65-856e-54854db3bba6\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"946to\\\\\\"><i>Save the file (control x) and type sudo reboot to restart.</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e7uou\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"15\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Changing the desktop environment with the command line\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"clsei\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1rh6j\\\\\\"><i>The kiosk user will now automatically boot into an Ubuntu gnome session. From here you can go into the settings to turn automatic login off, or really change anything else you'd like.</i></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"e93uc\\\\\\"><i>Bonus: Set the Kiosks to Shutdown at closing time</i></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bhek9\\\\\\"><i>To set Ubuntu to shutdown at specific times of the day you can use cron. There is plenty of documentation on cron so I'll just leave this snippet below to shutdown the kiosk at 6pm Monday through Friday.</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"65uek\\\\\\"><i>using the administrator account enter the following:</i></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a4301d7a-8a0c-47d9-9a24-ca21bc050087\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo crontab -e\\", \\"id\\": \\"4d6ce59a-6308-459a-8bc4-c5d70edcb266\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kyxtn\\\\\\">(If you're asked which editor to use, select 1 for nano)</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8oq40\\\\\\">Enter the following at the bottom of the file:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"af6f9b07-7b76-4db5-855f-c4d3b8351a0e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"0 18 * * 1-5 /sbin/shutdown -h now\\", \\"id\\": \\"488c68f4-74c3-4ed9-b463-b7a4842035cf\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"rfhfo\\\\\\">OK it only took 5 months but I finally finished my second blog post. This one was a bit of a doozy. If you've made it to the end, congratulations! And thank you!</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"d59931d7-d63f-49ba-b1e5-79f8d82d3e36\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 16, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 207, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 207, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": "2025-06-25T06:30:14.908Z", "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-25T06:30:14.850Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
152	2025-06-20 03:53:14.683467+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bu0hm\\\\\\"><b>Step 4:</b> Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">create the gnome-kiosk-script for your user by entering in a terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"igkce\\\\\\">Install pyqt5 and test launch the kiosk browser</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6n6eq\\\\\\">I've developed this kiosk browser using pyqt5. With PyQt5 you can easily build your own chromium based browser experience with all the bells and whistles you need. For the purposes of this tutorial I'll show you how to download and install my project. The intricacies of coding in Python are out of scope for this tutorial.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cdlro\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"br1iv\\\\\\">Install python3-pip, which is used to install external python dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pip</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"8l3s7\\\\\\">Install git with sudo apt install git</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"a63lr\\\\\\">Install dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pyqt5 python3-pyqt5.qtwebengine</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"66cdi\\\\\\">Download the kiosk browser code from gitlab: git clone https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"5gp0s\\\\\\">Open the file kiosk-webpage.txt. Replace the duckduckgo search engine with the webpage of your choice.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3je9i\\\\\\">Launch the browser: python kiosk-browser.py. When you're finished you can hit Ctrl + c to exit the terminal process.</li></ol>\\", \\"id\\": \\"78f6be1f-c6c1-4a09-8554-6910c18837d2\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"eh3tl\\\\\\">Auto Launch the Browser in Kiosk Mode</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9e3d3\\\\\\">We'll want the kiosk browser to load on boot. To do this, we need to edit gnome-kiosk configuration file we created earlier. In the terminal enter the following command:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"7d03e951-3a7c-4f01-9da6-cd90f9f8acde\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"nano /.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"752161b5-2183-4d66-9fe4-28fb2bea854b\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"o8ojl\\\\\\">put a # in front of the line gnome-editor ~/.local/bin/gnome-kiosk-script (or just delete it). Then add the following lines:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e6t5m\\\\\\">cd ~/kiosk-browser</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"58sht\\\\\\">python3 kiosk-browser.py</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"8\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"How your gnome-kiosk-script file should look after editing\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bhmi6\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"49a1681f-8759-4f04-8bb4-5a9f9f056f41\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 151, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-20T03:52:46.476Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
153	2025-06-20 03:54:50.758253+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p da