"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"igkce\\\\\\">Install pyqt5 and test launch the kiosk browser</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6n6eq\\\\\\">I've developed this kiosk browser using pyqt5. With PyQt5 you can easily build your own chromium based browser experience with all the bells and whistles you need. For the purposes of this tutorial I'll show you how to download and install my project. The intricacies of coding in Python are out of scope for this tutorial,.</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cdlro\\\\\\">Open the terminal and ensure python3 is working by typing: python3. You can type exit() to leave the python shell. If python3 is not install you can install it with: sudo apt install python3.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"br1iv\\\\\\">Install python3-pip, which is used to install external python dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pip</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"8l3s7\\\\\\">Install git with sudo apt install git</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"a63lr\\\\\\">Install dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pyqt5 python3-pyqt5.qtwebengine</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"66cdi\\\\\\">Download the repository from gitlab: git clone <a href=\\\\\\"https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser\\\\\\">https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser</a></li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"5gp0s\\\\\\">Open the file kiosk-webpage.txt. Replace the duckduckgo search engine with the webpage of your choice.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3je9i\\\\\\">Launch the browser: python kiosk-browser.py. When you're finished you can hit Ctrl + c to exit the terminal process. </li></ol>\\", \\"id\\": \\"78f6be1f-c6c1-4a09-8554-6910c18837d2\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eh3tl\\\\\\">Now that </p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"7d03e951-3a7c-4f01-9da6-cd90f9f8acde\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 145, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-20T03:26:45.359Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
143	2025-06-19 05:28:38.74113+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bu0hm\\\\\\"><b>Step 4:</b> Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">create the gnome-kiosk-script for your user by entering in a terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"igkce\\\\\\">Install pyqt5 and launch the kiosk browser</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6n6eq\\\\\\">I've developed this kiosk browser using pyqt5. With PyQt5 you can easily build your own chromium based browser experience with all the bells and whistles you need. For the purposes of this tutorial I'll show you how to download and install my project. The intricacies of coding in Python are out of scope for this tutorial,.  </p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cdlro\\\\\\">Open the terminal and ensure python3 is working by typing: python3. You can type exit() to leave the python shell. If python3 is not install you can install it with: sudo apt install python3.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"br1iv\\\\\\">Install python3-pip, which is used to install external python dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pip</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"8l3s7\\\\\\">Install git with sudo apt install git</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"a63lr\\\\\\">Install dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pyqt5 python3-pyqt5.qtwebengine</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"66cdi\\\\\\">Download the repository from gitlab: git clone <a href=\\\\\\"https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser\\\\\\">https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser</a></li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"5gp0s\\\\\\">Open the file kiosk-webpage.txt. Replace the duckduckgo search engine with the webpage of your choice.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3je9i\\\\\\">Launch the browser: python kiosk-browser.py</li></ol>\\", \\"id\\": \\"78f6be1f-c6c1-4a09-8554-6910c18837d2\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 142, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-19T05:13:21.738Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
144	2025-06-19 05:31:28.124312+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bu0hm\\\\\\"><b>Step 4:</b> Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">create the gnome-kiosk-script for your user by entering in a terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"igkce\\\\\\">Install pyqt5 and launch the kiosk browser</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6n6eq\\\\\\">I've developed this kiosk browser using pyqt5. With PyQt5 you can easily build your own chromium based browser experience with all the bells and whistles you need. For the purposes of this tutorial I'll show you how to download and install my project. The intricacies of coding in Python are out of scope for this tutorial,.</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cdlro\\\\\\">Open the terminal and ensure python3 is working by typing: python3. You can type exit() to leave the python shell. If python3 is not install you can install it with: sudo apt install python3.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"br1iv\\\\\\">Install python3-pip, which is used to install external python dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pip</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"8l3s7\\\\\\">Install git with sudo apt install git</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"a63lr\\\\\\">Install dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pyqt5 python3-pyqt5.qtwebengine</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"66cdi\\\\\\">Download the repository from gitlab: git clone <a href=\\\\\\"https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser\\\\\\">https://gitlab.com/pentictonlibrary/kiosk-browser</a></li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"5gp0s\\\\\\">Open the file kiosk-webpage.txt. Replace the duckduckgo search engine with the webpage of your choice.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3je9i\\\\\\">Launch the browser: python kiosk-browser.py</li></ol>\\", \\"id\\": \\"78f6be1f-c6c1-4a09-8554-6910c18837d2\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 143, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-19T05:28:38.741Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
