ta-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 137, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-19T04:53:20.584Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
139	2025-06-19 05:05:19.450987+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 138, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-19T05:05:05.348Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
140	2025-06-19 05:08:49.66824+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bu0hm\\\\\\"><b>Step 4: </b>Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk. </p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">create the gnome-kiosk-script for your user by entering in a terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly. </p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 139, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-19T05:05:19.450Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
141	2025-06-19 05:09:59.687748+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bu0hm\\\\\\"><b>Step 4:</b> Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">create the gnome-kiosk-script for your user by entering in a terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 140, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-19T05:08:49.668Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
142	2025-06-19 05:13:21.738052+00	{"pk": 8, "live": false, "path": "000100020002", "slug": "making-a-kiosk", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Making a Kiosk", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dbeyi\\\\\\">A few months ago I tried to install the free version of Porteus Kiosk, an operating system designed to provide a restricted browser experience, such as browsing a store's inventory. Unfortunately, the latest version of Porteus didn't offer a freemium option, and previous versions had been completely scrubbed from the website. Rather than sign up for another subscription service, I decided to build my own open source alternative using Ubuntu Linux, Gnome-Kiosk, and Pyqt5.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nsfu\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"auc7p\\\\\\">Goals:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7cou4\\\\\\">The public should only have access to a full screen web browser</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"29e9h\\\\\\">The web browser experience should be limited to a few websites</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lact\\\\\\">Accessibility features such as zoom in and zoom out</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9au5h\\\\\\">An inactivity timer would clear the cache and reset the browser back to the defined homepage</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"50nkh\\\\\\"><b>NOTE:</b> As of this writing <a href=\\\\\\"https://github.com/GNOME/gnome-kiosk\\\\\\">Gnome Kiosk</a> is still in early development and isn't available in most Linux repositories. I had the most success using Ubuntu 24.04, although it is also available on Fedora based systems. Speaking of Fedora, my project was largely inspired by this <a href=\\\\\\"https://fedoramagazine.org/build-a-kiosk-with-fedora-silverblue/\\\\\\">blog post</a> by Fedora Magazine. My preference for Ubuntu related to an auto logon issue, but I'll spare you the details. I had success on x64 machines; the Raspberry pi was a bit of a fail. Your millage may vary.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"brmv2\\\\\\"><b>Assumptions:</b> You should know how to install Linux based systems and be a little-bit familiar with the command line.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30qd8\\\\\\"><b>Step 1:</b> Download and Install Ubuntu Desktop with a user called kiosk (or any other name that you like). There are plenty of tutorials on the Internet (including chatGPT) on how to do that. Hint: If you're trying this on actual hardware create bootable Linux USB stick and make sure to disable secure boot.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c926k\\\\\\"><b>Step 2:</b> Run the software updater program to ensure your running the latest software and utilities on Ubuntu.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aadkv\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"669e60ed-7936-4ec1-88d3-90acd3893297\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade\\", \\"id\\": \\"6a69ab7f-c48a-4e86-a853-ada30eb14c48\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3x3bz\\\\\\"><b>Step 3:</b> Create another user called Administrator. You can do this by hitting super key --&gt; Type settings --&gt; System --&gt; Users. Make sure the Administrator toggle is turned on. This account serve as a fallback to make changes after we take admin privileges away from the kiosk account later on.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bu0hm\\\\\\"><b>Step 4:</b> Install gnome kiosk. Open the terminal and type the following to install gnome kiosk.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a2ff1026-09a1-4061-800b-b349f1db6fb4\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"sudo apt install gnome-kiosk\\", \\"id\\": \\"1ff2d303-7e53-48e7-bf6b-f52d2ac8a910\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">create the gnome-kiosk-script for your user by entering in a terminal:</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"a3ad4b3f-2b78-4366-90f0-d990e6edc829\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"code_snippet\\", \\"value\\": \\"gnome-kiosk-script\\", \\"id\\": \\"a5f40dd7-9ced-41e1-97a9-d8b94f03ab2e\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"0exi5\\\\\\">A configuration file will appear. You can x out of it and hit control + c to exit. We'll edit this configuration file shortly.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"060b5aad-3d27-4765-8053-95632239050d\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"igkce\\\\\\">Install pyqt5 and launch the kiosk browser</h2><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6n6eq\\\\\\">Open the terminal and ensure python3 is working by typing: python3. You can type exit() to leave the python shell. If python3 is not install you can install it with: sudo apt install python3.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"br1iv\\\\\\">Install python3-pip, which is used to install external python dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pip </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"a63lr\\\\\\">Install dependencies: sudo apt install python3-pyqt5 python3-pyqt5.qtwebengine </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"5gp0s\\\\\\">Open the file kiosk-webpage.txt. Replace the duckduckgo search engine with the webpage of your choice.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3je9i\\\\\\">Launch the browser: python kiosk-browser.py</li></ol>\\", \\"id\\": \\"78f6be1f-c6c1-4a09-8554-6910c18837d2\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/making-a-kiosk/", "blog_date": "2025-01-12", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 7, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Making a Kiosk", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 141, "translation_key": "f9eedea1-e68f-478c-8625-cae0c2c61560", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": "2025-01-13T07:30:46.586Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-06-19T05:09:59.687Z"}	\N	8	1	44	1	Making a Kiosk
607	2026-06-21 17:50:32.755333+00	{"pk": 12, "live": true, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sc27\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3bug\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbidb\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6h8v7\\\\\\">Between 2020 and 2021 the Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner, in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos, including those of children, from social media platforms to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"f872d\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1thfg\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Google Trends from the last two years (May 2024 to May 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV), with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"image\\", \\"value\\": 36, \\"id\\": \\"c3e479dc-1c89-404c-96b8-1ef74c27f175\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"grie9\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9kbb\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act). <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/story/us-vs-microsoft-supreme-court-case-data/\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine states</a>: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2aot0\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As they put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8otdg\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7bqd0\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ebqq6\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to interfere with and undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aneus\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence pivotal decisions, including Brexit, as well as election in countries such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"46spi\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after he was sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"69g1d\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing. As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>, Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising under-explored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dufel\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d8ld8\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy cannot happen without viable alternatives to U.S.-dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5941n\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to Dropbox. As of this writing they boast <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a78ol\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of alternative technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. My theory is that people simply won't choose to use an alternative product solely on the basis that it prioritized their privacy. Rather users tend to choose the most intuitive product that helps them achieve their goals with the least amount of friction. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries or a salad? Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries fill you up faster, and they just taste better. Sure you could choose a software like <a href=\\\\\\"https://jitsi.org/jitsi-meet/\\\\\\">Jitsi Meet</a>, but Microsoft Teams already integrates with your calendar and you have three minutes before that meeting starts.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kshv\\\\\\">[Full disclosure I've never actually tried Jitsi Meet. I cast no shade on their hard work.]</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1rq40\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ef30f\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily morning routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my apartment. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but from their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalize vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not just a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"48c15813-6ee2-4e5a-9708-3b1f992e3940\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-06-22", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 37, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 605, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 606, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": "2026-06-21T17:11:17.750Z", "first_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-21T17:50:30.299Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
357	2026-06-01 19:51:44.972006+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. When you pick up your device in the morning, who's rules do you need to follow? Is it the country in which you live - the one you are expected to actively participate in, or is it another government's, one in which you had no hand in shaping? Unfortunately, some of this would be better answered by a legal scholar, but there are a few undisputable facts:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aucbu\\\\\\">Most internet technology, be it social media, smartphone, AI, and e-commerce are dominated by a handful United States companies (Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Google), and come under US jurisdiction.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"eau8t\\\\\\">While there have been a number of concerning trends, one of the most egregious is The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centers located in the European Union.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"ec7fe\\\\\\">The US Law is shaped by private interest, not the interest of its citizens, save little about citizens who live outside the United States. If this sounds conspiratorial to you, let me point you to this graph:</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pd5c\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know\\\\\\">https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eui29\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7fbgv\\\\\\">Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2b671\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5eahb\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 357, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-01T19:51:29.066Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
396	2026-06-07 17:52:48.852658+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), had I also been subject to U.S. laws, perhaps before I even got out of bed?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking  when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">Going back to my morning ritual, when I wake up, I'm in Canada, which last time I checked was the democratic society in which I live. We Canadians naturally expect that we get to play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev0s8\\\\\\">But as soon as I pick my device, I'm transported to When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 396, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T17:52:46.360Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
603	2026-06-21 17:10:20.431339+00	{"pk": 12, "live": true, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sc27\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3bug\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbidb\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6h8v7\\\\\\">Between 2020 and 2021 the Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner, in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos, including those of children, from social media platforms to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"f872d\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1thfg\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Google Trends from the last two years (May 2024 to May 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV), with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1tbqo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b0e9n\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f6fb3\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e7c3e\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act). <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/story/us-vs-microsoft-supreme-court-case-data/\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine states</a>: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"18r4k\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As they put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"54hnd\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30m8c\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"10v7g\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to interfere with and undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e5bo4\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence pivotal decisions, including Brexit, as well as election in countries such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4paii\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after he was sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"add30\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing. As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>, Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising under-explored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2lspo\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9tc28\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy cannot happen without viable alternatives to U.S.-dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r95o\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to Dropbox. As of this writing they boast <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6tp\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of alternative technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. My theory is that people simply won't choose to use an alternative product solely on the basis that it prioritized their privacy. Rather users tend to choose the most intuitive product that helps them achieve their goals with the least amount of friction. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries or a salad? Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries fill you up faster, and they just taste better. Sure you could choose a software like <a href=\\\\\\"https://jitsi.org/jitsi-meet/\\\\\\">Jitsi Meet</a>, but Microsoft Teams already integrates with your calendar and you have three minutes before that meeting starts.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e41dk\\\\\\">[Full disclosure I've never actually tried Jitsi Meet. I cast no shade on their hard work.]</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"55ngi\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"52mv3\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily morning routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my apartment. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but from their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalize vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not just a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-06-22", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 37, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 602, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 603, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:36:11.437Z", "first_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-21T17:10:18.942Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
601	2026-06-21 00:36:08.494033+00	{"pk": 12, "live": true, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sc27\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3bug\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbidb\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6h8v7\\\\\\">Between 2020 and 2021 the Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner, in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos, including those of children, from social media platforms to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"f872d\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1thfg\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Google Trends from the last two years (May 2024 to May 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV), with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1tbqo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b0e9n\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f6fb3\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e7c3e\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act). <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/story/us-vs-microsoft-supreme-court-case-data/\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine states</a>: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"18r4k\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As they put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"54hnd\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30m8c\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"10v7g\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c9fqc\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to interfere with and undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e5bo4\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence pivotal decisions, including Brexit, as well as election in countries such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4paii\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after he was sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"add30\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing. As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>, Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising under-explored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2lspo\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9tc28\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy cannot happen without viable alternatives to U.S.-dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r95o\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to Dropbox. As of this writing they boast <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6tp\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of alternative technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. My theory is that people simply won't choose to use an alternative product solely on the basis that it prioritized their privacy. Rather users tend to choose the most intuitive product that helps them achieve their goals with the least amount of friction. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries or a salad Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries fill you up faster, and they just taste better. Sure you could choose a software like <a href=\\\\\\"https://jitsi.org/jitsi-meet/\\\\\\">Jitsi Meet</a>, but Microsoft Teams already integrates with your calendar and you have three minutes before that meeting starts.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e41dk\\\\\\">[Full disclosure I've never actually tried Jitsi Meet. I cast no shade on their hard work.]</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"55ngi\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"52mv3\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily morning routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my apartment. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but from their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalize vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not just a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-06-22", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 37, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 600, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 600, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:28:21.566Z", "first_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-21T00:28:21.514Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
327	2026-05-25 02:09:09.670395+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. <b>Lets briefly discuss some pre-2025 things.</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du7rk\\\\\\"><b>Not a New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fhsjo\\\\\\">All the way back in 2004 the City of Munich approved <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.theregister.com/software/2004/06/17/munich-embraces-the-penguin/1278077\\\\\\">a transition to Linux</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36tfh\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo\\\\\\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1lfip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9qj9k\\\\\\"><b>France National Police Force</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"><b>Now let's discuss 2025 to present</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9stp5\\\\\\"><b>Let's make some dangerous predictions</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2t9rd\\\\\\"><b>Talk about the flavour rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u3i4\\\\\\"><b>Where you want to go next</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu\\\\\\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7k24j\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/2013/09/gendarmerie-linux/\\\\\\">https://www.wired.com/2013/09/gendarmerie-linux/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 326, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-25T02:06:59.959Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
612	2026-06-22 17:07:16.731102+00	{"pk": 12, "live": true, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sc27\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3bug\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbidb\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6h8v7\\\\\\">Between 2020 and 2021 the Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner, in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos, including those of children, from social media platforms to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"f872d\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1thfg\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Google Trends from the last two years (May 2024 to May 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV), with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"image\\", \\"value\\": 36, \\"id\\": \\"c3e479dc-1c89-404c-96b8-1ef74c27f175\\"}, {\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"grie9\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9kbb\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act). <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/story/us-vs-microsoft-supreme-court-case-data/\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine states</a>: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2aot0\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As they put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8otdg\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7bqd0\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ebqq6\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to interfere with and undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aneus\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence pivotal decisions, including Brexit, as well as election in countries such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"46spi\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after he was sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"69g1d\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing. As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>, Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising under-explored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dufel\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d8ld8\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy cannot happen without viable alternatives to U.S.-dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5941n\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to Dropbox. As of this writing they boast <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a78ol\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of alternative technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. My theory is that people simply won't choose to use an alternative product solely on the basis that it prioritized their privacy. Rather users tend to choose the most intuitive product that helps them achieve their goals with the least amount of friction. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries or a salad? Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries fill you up faster, and they just taste better. Sure you could choose a software like <a href=\\\\\\"https://jitsi.org/jitsi-meet/\\\\\\">Jitsi Meet</a>, but Microsoft Teams already integrates with your calendar and you have three minutes before that meeting starts.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kshv\\\\\\">[Full disclosure I've never actually tried Jitsi Meet. I cast no shade on their hard work.]</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1rq40\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ef30f\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily morning routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my apartment. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but from their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalize vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not just a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"48c15813-6ee2-4e5a-9708-3b1f992e3940\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-06-22", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "blog_image": 37, "go_live_at": null, "blog_teaser": "", "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 611, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 612, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": "2026-06-21T18:35:58.100Z", "first_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "search_description": "Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. When thinking about the societies in which we want to live, it's an essential piece to consider.", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-22T17:07:10.826Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
