253	2026-01-10 19:30:27.277004+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cl6qe\\\\\\">Poolish: In a large bowl add the whole wheat flour and bread flour. Then add the water and sourdough starter.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter: </b>Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> on how to make one. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"hbpc\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 252, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T19:28:41.263Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
258	2026-01-10 20:02:18.645205+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9k7b0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the receipe.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"hbpc\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 257, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T20:00:48.099Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
266	2026-01-17 21:40:26.197107+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9k7b0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping and Waiting:</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\"></p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"604vs\\\\\\"><b>Boiling:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 265, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T21:39:56.779Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
602	2026-06-21 00:36:11.373895+00	{"pk": 12, "live": true, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sc27\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3bug\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbidb\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6h8v7\\\\\\">Between 2020 and 2021 the Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner, in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos, including those of children, from social media platforms to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"f872d\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1thfg\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Google Trends from the last two years (May 2024 to May 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV), with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1tbqo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b0e9n\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f6fb3\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e7c3e\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act). <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/story/us-vs-microsoft-supreme-court-case-data/\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine states</a>: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"18r4k\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As they put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"54hnd\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30m8c\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"10v7g\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c9fqc\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to interfere with and undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e5bo4\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence pivotal decisions, including Brexit, as well as election in countries such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4paii\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after he was sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"add30\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing. As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>, Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising under-explored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2lspo\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9tc28\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy cannot happen without viable alternatives to U.S.-dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r95o\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to Dropbox. As of this writing they boast <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6tp\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of alternative technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. My theory is that people simply won't choose to use an alternative product solely on the basis that it prioritized their privacy. Rather users tend to choose the most intuitive product that helps them achieve their goals with the least amount of friction. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries or a salad? Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries fill you up faster, and they just taste better. Sure you could choose a software like <a href=\\\\\\"https://jitsi.org/jitsi-meet/\\\\\\">Jitsi Meet</a>, but Microsoft Teams already integrates with your calendar and you have three minutes before that meeting starts.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e41dk\\\\\\">[Full disclosure I've never actually tried Jitsi Meet. I cast no shade on their hard work.]</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"55ngi\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"52mv3\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily morning routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my apartment. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but from their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalize vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not just a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-06-22", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 37, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 600, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 601, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:28:21.566Z", "first_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-21T00:36:08.494Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
599	2026-06-21 00:28:19.406284+00	{"pk": 12, "live": true, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sc27\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3bug\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbidb\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6h8v7\\\\\\">Between 2020 and 2021 the Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner, in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos, including those of children, from social media platforms to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"f872d\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1thfg\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Google Trends from the last two years (May 2024 to May 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV), with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1tbqo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b0e9n\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f6fb3\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e7c3e\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act). <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/story/us-vs-microsoft-supreme-court-case-data/\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine states</a>: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"18r4k\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As they put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"54hnd\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30m8c\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"10v7g\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c9fqc\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to interfere with and undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e5bo4\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence pivotal decisions, including Brexit, as well as election in countries such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4paii\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after he was sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"add30\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing. As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>, Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising under-explored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2lspo\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9tc28\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy cannot happen without viable alternatives to U.S.-dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r95o\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to Dropbox. As of this writing they boast <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6tp\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of alternative technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. My theory is that people simply won't choose to use an alternative product solely on the basis that it prioritized their privacy. Rather users tend to choose the most intuitive product that helps them achieve their goals with the least amount of friction. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries fill you up faster, and they just taste better. Sure you could choose a software like <a href=\\\\\\"https://jitsi.org/jitsi-meet/\\\\\\">Jitsi Meet</a>, but Microsoft Teams already integrates with your calendar and you have three minutes before that meeting starts.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e41dk\\\\\\">[Full disclosure I've never actually tried Jitsi Meet. I cast no shade on their hard work.]</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"55ngi\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"52mv3\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily morning routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my apartment. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but from their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalize vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not just a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-06-22", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 37, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 598, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 599, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "first_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-21T00:28:13.909Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
