data-block-key=\\\\\\"c9fqc\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to interfere with and undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e5bo4\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence pivotal decisions, including Brexit, as well as election in countries such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4paii\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after he was sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"add30\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing. As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>, Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising under-explored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2lspo\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9tc28\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy cannot happen without viable alternatives to U.S.-dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r95o\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to Dropbox. As of this writing they boast <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6tp\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of alternative technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. My theory is that people simply won't choose to use an alternative product solely on the basis that it prioritized their privacy. Rather users tend to choose the most intuitive product that helps them achieve their goals with the least amount of friction. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries fill you up faster, and they just taste better. Sure you could choose a software like <a href=\\\\\\"https://jitsi.org/jitsi-meet/\\\\\\">Jitsi Meet</a>, but Microsoft Teams already integrates with your calendar and you have three minutes before that meeting starts.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e41dk\\\\\\">[Full disclosure I've never actually tried Jitsi Meet. I cast no shade on their hard work.]</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"55ngi\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"52mv3\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily morning routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my apartment. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but from their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalize vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not just a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-06-22", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 37, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 598, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 599, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "first_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-21T00:28:19.406Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
605	2026-06-21 17:11:17.667815+00	{"pk": 12, "live": true, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sc27\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3bug\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbidb\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6h8v7\\\\\\">Between 2020 and 2021 the Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner, in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos, including those of children, from social media platforms to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"f872d\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1thfg\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Google Trends from the last two years (May 2024 to May 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV), with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1tbqo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b0e9n\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f6fb3\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e7c3e\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act). <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/story/us-vs-microsoft-supreme-court-case-data/\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine states</a>: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"18r4k\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As they put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"54hnd\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"30m8c\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"10v7g\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to interfere with and undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e5bo4\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence pivotal decisions, including Brexit, as well as election in countries such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4paii\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after he was sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"add30\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing. As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>, Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising under-explored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2lspo\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9tc28\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy cannot happen without viable alternatives to U.S.-dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r95o\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to Dropbox. As of this writing they boast <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8n6tp\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of alternative technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. My theory is that people simply won't choose to use an alternative product solely on the basis that it prioritized their privacy. Rather users tend to choose the most intuitive product that helps them achieve their goals with the least amount of friction. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries or a salad? Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries fill you up faster, and they just taste better. Sure you could choose a software like <a href=\\\\\\"https://jitsi.org/jitsi-meet/\\\\\\">Jitsi Meet</a>, but Microsoft Teams already integrates with your calendar and you have three minutes before that meeting starts.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e41dk\\\\\\">[Full disclosure I've never actually tried Jitsi Meet. I cast no shade on their hard work.]</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"55ngi\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"52mv3\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily morning routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my apartment. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but from their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalize vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not just a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-06-22", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 37, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 602, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 604, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:36:11.437Z", "first_published_at": "2026-06-21T00:26:12.862Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-21T17:10:21.338Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
376	2026-06-07 04:52:18.001386+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups, violin practice while the watering plants, but the reality is more like hitting snooze on my alarm, watching a YouTube short, and a slog to the coffee pot. If I have enough impulse control to ignore the onslaught my social media algorithm, I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading off to work. The question I keep asking myself: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), who\\\\u2019s legal jurisdiction was I in while using their products?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">Before I go any further I should state that I am a Librarian by training, and not a lawyer, <i>never mind a legal scholar.</i> Nonetheless I can state three facts that demonstrate digital sovereignty is increasing interest for individuals, organisations and nations states. </p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d3jsi\\\\\\">There have been many red flags that have demonstrated why we need a refuge from massive American tech companies. For me personally the tipping point ocured, watching Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attend the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a1in\\\\\\">The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a6dbk\\\\\\"><b>New Alternatives, New Opportunities</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 375, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T04:52:15.688Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
469	2026-06-14 19:53:11.105269+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one. This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement internationally, known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce. In truth, alternatives are everyone, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. There has been a lot written about why one type of product becomes more widely adopted another. I'll write about this more in a later post, suffice to say, if digital sovereignty is to succeed, the alternatives need to easier to use then their competitors. When describing bread, legendary Baker Peter Reinhart once talked about what he called 'The Flavour Rule', that is, it didn't matter how much healthier one bread was over another, people would also chose the bread that tasted the best. The goal to making a healthier bread was to make it taste  </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5j6ts\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 469, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T19:53:09.106Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
309	2026-05-24 20:38:29.124697+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States (AKA, the Orange Dude).  Some of the stated objectives of the new regime included Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: The United States, once considered by many to be a beacon of freedom and democracy in the world, was now an authoritarian power ready to brute force its dominance against anyone it saw as ripe for exploitation. The Tech CEOs, clearly tired of virtue signalising pluralistic values were along for the ride, acting as defacto Praetorian Guard. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this moment was about to usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex, with a mariad of new innovations and solutions supported by middle powers, the open source community, and new players trying to enter the market.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7kupm\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 308, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T20:38:28.805Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
321	2026-05-25 02:02:29.28507+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. <b>Lets briefly discuss some pre-2025 things.</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du7rk\\\\\\"><b>Not a New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fhsjo\\\\\\">All the way back in 2004 the City of Munich approved <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.theregister.com/software/2004/06/17/munich-embraces-the-penguin/1278077\\\\\\">a transition to Linux</a>. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36tfh\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo\\\\\\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1lfip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"><b>Now let's discuss 2025 to present</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9stp5\\\\\\"><b>Let's make some dangerous predictions</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2t9rd\\\\\\"><b>Talk about the flavour rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u3i4\\\\\\"><b>Where you want to go next</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 320, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-25T02:02:26.965Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
335	2026-05-25 19:56:03.589637+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology. I know I felt that way. But the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. <b>Lets briefly discuss some pre-2025 things.</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3lq6h\\\\\\"><b>So What is Digital Sovereignty?</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"10iio\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.lemonde.fr/en/summer-reads/article/2025/07/20/the-day-silicon-valley-s-oligarchs-kissed-trump-s-ring_6743557_183.html\\\\\\">https://www.lemonde.fr/en/summer-reads/article/2025/07/20/the-day-silicon-valley-s-oligarchs-kissed-trump-s-ring_6743557_183.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du7rk\\\\\\"><b>Not a New Concern</b></p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fhsjo\\\\\\">All the way back in 2004 the City of Munich approved <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.theregister.com/software/2004/06/17/munich-embraces-the-penguin/1278077\\\\\\">a transition to Linux</a>. citing the extensive cost of Microsoft Licensing.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cil48\\\\\\">That US legislation that basically states that the US Gov can request user information from another country as long as the company is American, even if the software is hosted internationally</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36tfh\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo\\\\\\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1lfip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9qj9k\\\\\\"><b>France National Police Force</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"><b>Now let's discuss 2025 to present</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9stp5\\\\\\"><b>Let's make some dangerous predictions</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2t9rd\\\\\\"><b>Talk about the flavour rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u3i4\\\\\\"><b>Where you want to go next</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu\\\\\\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7k24j\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/2013/09/gendarmerie-linux/\\\\\\">https://www.wired.com/2013/09/gendarmerie-linux/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 334, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-25T19:55:53.022Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
358	2026-06-01 19:54:19.802684+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. When you pick up your device in the morning, who's rules do you need to follow? Is it the country in which you live - the one you are expected to actively participate in, or is it another government's, one in which you had no hand in shaping? Unfortunately, some of this would be better answered by a legal scholar, but there are a few undisputable facts:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aucbu\\\\\\">Most internet technology, be it social media, smartphone, AI, and e-commerce are dominated by a handful United States companies (Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Google), and come under US jurisdiction.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"eau8t\\\\\\">While there have been a number of concerning trends, one of the most egregious is The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centers located in the European Union.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"ec7fe\\\\\\">The US Law is shaped by private interest, not the interest of its citizens, save little about citizens who live outside the United States. If this sounds conspiratorial to you, let me point you to this graph:</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pd5c\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know\\\\\\">https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eui29\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7fbgv\\\\\\">Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2b671\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5eahb\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 357, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-01T19:51:44.972Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
364	2026-06-03 22:02:24.582216+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">When you pick up your device in the morning, who's rules do you need to follow? Is it the country in which you live - the one you are expected to actively participate in, or is it another government's, one in which you had no hand in shaping? In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. Unfortunately, some of this would be better answered by a legal scholar, but there are a few undisputed facts:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aucbu\\\\\\">Most internet technology, be it social media, smartphone, AI, and e-commerce are dominated by a handful United States companies (Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Google), and come under US jurisdiction.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"eau8t\\\\\\">While there have been a number of concerning trends, one of the most egregious is The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centers located in the European Union.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"ec7fe\\\\\\">The US Law is shaped by private interest, not the interest of its citizens, save little about citizens who live outside the United States. If this sounds conspiratorial to you, let me point you to this graph:</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pd5c\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know\\\\\\">https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eui29\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7fbgv\\\\\\">Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2b671\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5eahb\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 363, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-03T22:02:22.317Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
384	2026-06-07 16:53:48.667415+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups, violin practice while the watering plants, but the reality is more like hitting snooze on my alarm, watching a YouTube short, and a slog to the coffee pot. If I have enough impulse control to ignore the onslaught my social media algorithm, I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. The question I keep asking myself: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), who\\\\u2019s legal jurisdiction was I in while using their products?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">Before I go any further I should state that I am a librarian not a lawyer. Nonetheless I can state three facts that demonstrate digital sovereignty is increasing interest for individuals, organisations and nations states.</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d3jsi\\\\\\">There have been many red flags that have demonstrated why we need a refuge from massive American tech companies. For me personally the tipping point occurred when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. But for many, the realisation occurred much earlier with the passage of the 2018 U.S. law, The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>).</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">Going back to my morning ritual, when I wake up, I'm in Canada, which last time I checked was the democratic society in which I live. We Canadians naturally expect that we get to play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev0s8\\\\\\">But as soon as I pick my device, I'm transported to When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 383, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T16:53:14.614Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
572	2026-06-20 19:29:39.506776+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunties for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">I began this post  by describing my daily routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's  jurisdiction before fully getting out of bed. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 572, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:29:36.004Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
463	2026-06-14 19:27:12.249242+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one. This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement internationally, known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 462, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T19:26:25.532Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
1	2024-08-06 18:23:49.480573+00	{"pk": 4, "live": true, "path": "00010002", "slug": "welcome-to-my-website", "depth": 2, "intro": "<p data-block-key=\\"eric2\\">Your place for all the latest and greatest Dan Lerch news on the internet.</p>", "owner": 1, "title": "Welcome to My Website", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome-to-my-website/", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Welcome to My Website", "content_type": 43, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": null, "translation_key": "ca41e4fb-cd08-44bf-b885-629482ea1b91", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": null}	\N	4	1	43	1	Welcome to My Website
283	2026-01-17 23:05:39.356916+00	{"pk": 11, "live": true, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\">1) Using a knife, divide the dough into 90 gram pieces and shape into round dough balls. Place each dough ball on a baking sheet and keep covered. I recommend lining the baking sheet with parchment to prevent sticking. I also use small garbage bags to cover rather that plastic wrap, but that's entirely up to you.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"88i8u\\\\\\">2) Roll each ball into a 30 cm (1 foot) sausage-like strip. I find it easier to apply pressure from the centre, working my way to the outside.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8slqk\\\\\\">3) Push the ends together and then roll on your work surface until you've formed the final bagel shape.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5ei\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> It is essential to keep any bagel you are not currently working on completely covered. The dough pieces can dry out very quickly making the dough much more difficult to work with.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"25\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagel Roll\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6631v\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"26\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Shapping the bagel into a sausage-like shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kg72\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"27\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Combing the ends of the bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fjaiv\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"28\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling the ends of the bagel to make a combined shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fle38\\\\\\">When finished shaping all 12 bagels, let rest at room temperature for approximately two hours until they gently risen. To test the proof, you can place a bagel in water to see if it floats. Admittedly, I rarely do this anymore.They should look similar to the images below.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"29\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9ibmo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"30\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"12 rolled bagel shapes on the tray\\\\\\"/><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n12f\\\\\\">Cold Fermentation</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3rvnd\\\\\\">Place your covered tray of bagels into the fridge and let cold ferment for at least 8 hours. This will dramatically improve the flavour and texture of the bagel, especially in the warmer months when dough rises much faster at room temperature.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"4lige\\\\\\">Preheat The Oven</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"69c4p\\\\\\">Preheat your oven to 500F (260C). If you have a baking stone it may take up to 45 minutes to get it to temperature.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"914v8\\\\\\">Boiling and Seasoning</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e6cnc\\\\\\">1) Pour some of your seasoning mix onto a plate.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bojnq\\\\\\">2) Bring a large pot of water to a boil with the baking soda (or baking powder), and the honey. *</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3g5be\\\\\\">*I usually choose a softer boil, not a rolling one, although I've seen many recipes that use this method. I find it easy enough to achieve the desired texture without destroying the dough. Also I recommend using a spider to hand the boiling process, but you can also use a large spoon.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4dbd3\\\\\\">3) Adding three bagels at a time boil each bagel for 2 minutes a side. When the four minutes are up, please each bagel into the season plate and cover both sides of the bagel. When finished you can place the bagels back on your baking sheet.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqjvb\\\\\\"></p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d9i5a\\\\\\">Place 6 bagels in the oven, then turn the heat down to 475F (245C). Bake for 6 minutes, flip the bagels over and bake for another 6 minutes. The bagels should have a nice char on them. If they don't cook on each side for an extra minute or two. When finished, place on a cooling rack and let sit for 20 minutes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f4sk\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> If using a baking stone, I recommend cleaning off the excess seeds between batches. I use a dough scraper and and plate. This prevents burnt seeds from getting onto your 2nd batch of bagels. Also Since the over door has been open for a bit, I usually put the temperate back at 500F (260C) for 8 minutes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7bnc5\\\\\\"><b>Serving:</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ljih\\\\\\">Don't be stingy with your cream cheese. This is an important rule. Slather it on. I prefer scallion cream cheese but you do you. I find the Liberte brand in Canada is the best, but you can't go wrong with Philadelphia. Experiment with what you look: lox, tomato, butter.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing Bagels</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f6475\\\\\\"><b>Short term:</b> I store bagels in a paper bag. Plastic bags seem to make the crust get soft. I have no idea why but this is my experience.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c6c1r\\\\\\"><b>Long Term:</b> You'll probably also notice that after about two days of room temperate storage the bagels start to stale. For longer-term storage I slice each bagel in half, place in paper bag. Then I place that paper bag in a zip-lock Freezer bag, and place in the freezer.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2026-01-17", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 282, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 282, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": "2026-01-17T23:04:34.151Z", "first_published_at": "2026-01-17T23:04:34.151Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T23:04:34.103Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
345	2026-06-01 18:53:00.251769+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">In 2018, The United States passed the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). To quote Wired Magazine: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centers located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e1vob\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5eahb\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 344, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-01T18:52:45.615Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
346	2026-06-01 18:54:22.780733+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">In 2018, The United States passed the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). To quote Wired Magazine: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centers located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e1vob\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6vpbj\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9hacc\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pd5c\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know\\\\\\">https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5eahb\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 345, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-01T18:53:00.251Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
322	2026-05-25 02:03:27.114824+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. <b>Lets briefly discuss some pre-2025 things.</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du7rk\\\\\\"><b>Not a New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fhsjo\\\\\\">All the way back in 2004 the City of Munich approved <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.theregister.com/software/2004/06/17/munich-embraces-the-penguin/1278077\\\\\\">a transition to Linux</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36tfh\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo\\\\\\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1lfip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"><b>Now let's discuss 2025 to present</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9stp5\\\\\\"><b>Let's make some dangerous predictions</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2t9rd\\\\\\"><b>Talk about the flavour rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u3i4\\\\\\"><b>Where you want to go next</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 321, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-25T02:02:29.285Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
359	2026-06-01 19:59:21.204908+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. When you pick up your device in the morning, who's rules do you need to follow? Is it the country in which you live - the one you are expected to actively participate in, or is it another government's, one in which you had no hand in shaping? Unfortunately, some of this would be better answered by a legal scholar, but there are a few undisputable facts:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aucbu\\\\\\">Most internet technology, be it social media, smartphone, AI, and e-commerce are dominated by a handful United States companies (Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Google), and come under US jurisdiction.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"eau8t\\\\\\">While there have been a number of concerning trends, one of the most egregious is The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centers located in the European Union.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"ec7fe\\\\\\">The US Law is shaped by private interest, not the interest of its citizens, save little about citizens who live outside the United States. If this sounds conspiratorial to you, let me point you to this graph:</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pd5c\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know\\\\\\">https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eui29\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7fbgv\\\\\\">Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2b671\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5eahb\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 358, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-01T19:54:19.802Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
365	2026-06-06 20:13:37.719826+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">When you pick up your device in the morning, who's rules do you need to follow? Is it the country in which you live - the one you are expected to actively participate in, or is it another government's, one in which you had no hand in shaping? In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. Unfortunately, some of this would be better answered by a legal scholar, but there are a few undisputed facts:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aucbu\\\\\\">Most internet technology, be it social media, smartphone, AI, and e-commerce are dominated by a handful United States companies (Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Google), and come under US jurisdiction.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"eau8t\\\\\\">While there have been a number of concerning trends, one of the most egregious is The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centers located in the European Union.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"ec7fe\\\\\\">The US Law is shaped by private interest, not the interest of its citizens, save little about citizens who live outside the United States. If this sounds conspiratorial to you, let me point you to this graph:</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pd5c\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know\\\\\\">https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eui29\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7fbgv\\\\\\">Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5iu6e\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5eahb\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 364, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-03T22:02:24.582Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
323	2026-05-25 02:05:42.194389+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. <b>Lets briefly discuss some pre-2025 things.</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du7rk\\\\\\"><b>Not a New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fhsjo\\\\\\">All the way back in 2004 the City of Munich approved <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.theregister.com/software/2004/06/17/munich-embraces-the-penguin/1278077\\\\\\">a transition to Linux</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36tfh\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo\\\\\\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1lfip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9qj9k\\\\\\"><b>France National Police Force</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"><b>Now let's discuss 2025 to present</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9stp5\\\\\\"><b>Let's make some dangerous predictions</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2t9rd\\\\\\"><b>Talk about the flavour rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u3i4\\\\\\"><b>Where you want to go next</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 323, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-25T02:05:38.228Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
324	2026-05-25 02:05:42.978282+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. <b>Lets briefly discuss some pre-2025 things.</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du7rk\\\\\\"><b>Not a New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fhsjo\\\\\\">All the way back in 2004 the City of Munich approved <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.theregister.com/software/2004/06/17/munich-embraces-the-penguin/1278077\\\\\\">a transition to Linux</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36tfh\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo\\\\\\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1lfip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9qj9k\\\\\\"><b>France National Police Force</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"><b>Now let's discuss 2025 to present</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9stp5\\\\\\"><b>Let's make some dangerous predictions</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2t9rd\\\\\\"><b>Talk about the flavour rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u3i4\\\\\\"><b>Where you want to go next</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 323, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-25T02:05:42.194Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
360	2026-06-02 02:35:10.790842+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. When you pick up your device in the morning, who's rules do you need to follow? Is it the country in which you live - the one you are expected to actively participate in, or is it another government's, one in which you had no hand in shaping? Unfortunately, some of this would be better answered by a legal scholar, but there are a few undisputed facts:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"aucbu\\\\\\">Most internet technology, be it social media, smartphone, AI, and e-commerce are dominated by a handful United States companies (Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Google), and come under US jurisdiction.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"eau8t\\\\\\">While there have been a number of concerning trends, one of the most egregious is The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centers located in the European Union.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"ec7fe\\\\\\">The US Law is shaped by private interest, not the interest of its citizens, save little about citizens who live outside the United States. If this sounds conspiratorial to you, let me point you to this graph:</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pd5c\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know\\\\\\">https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2026/04/data-sovereignty-and-the-cloud-act-what-canadian-organizations-should-know</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eui29\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7fbgv\\\\\\">Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2b671\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5eahb\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 359, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-01T19:59:21.204Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
385	2026-06-07 16:53:49.149908+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups, violin practice while the watering plants, but the reality is more like hitting snooze on my alarm, watching a YouTube short, and a slog to the coffee pot. If I have enough impulse control to ignore the onslaught my social media algorithm, I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. The question I keep asking myself: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), who\\\\u2019s legal jurisdiction was I in while using their products?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">Before I go any further I should state that I am a librarian not a lawyer. Nonetheless I can state three facts that demonstrate digital sovereignty is increasing interest for individuals, organisations and nations states.</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d3jsi\\\\\\">There have been many red flags that have demonstrated why we need a refuge from massive American tech companies. For me personally the tipping point occurred when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. But for many, the realisation occurred much earlier with the passage of the 2018 U.S. law, The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>).</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">Going back to my morning ritual, when I wake up, I'm in Canada, which last time I checked was the democratic society in which I live. We Canadians naturally expect that we get to play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev0s8\\\\\\">But as soon as I pick my device, I'm transported to When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 384, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T16:53:48.667Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
395	2026-06-07 17:33:32.530603+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), had I also been subject to U.S. laws, perhaps before I even got out of bed?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d3jsi\\\\\\">There have been many red flags that have demonstrated why we need a refuge from massive American tech companies. For me personally the tipping point occurred when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. But for many, the realisation occurred much earlier with the passage of the 2018 U.S. law, The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>).</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">Going back to my morning ritual, when I wake up, I'm in Canada, which last time I checked was the democratic society in which I live. We Canadians naturally expect that we get to play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev0s8\\\\\\">But as soon as I pick my device, I'm transported to When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 393, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T17:32:54.797Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
401	2026-06-07 17:59:31.812675+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), had I also been subject to U.S. laws, perhaps before I even got out of bed?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic proccesses around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Without sounding hyperbolic, U.S. Technology companies act as an oligarchy. That is, they use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 400, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T17:59:30.435Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
366	2026-06-07 01:21:27.121567+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">Hi Everyone,</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2phoc\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.  </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups, violin practice, and watering plants, but the reality is more like hitting snooze on my smart phone alarm, watching a YouTube short, and a slog to the coffee pot in order to make my synapses fire better. If I have enough impulse control to ignore social media algorithms, I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading off to work. The question I keep asking myself, if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), who\\\\u2019s legal jurisdiction was I in while using their products.  </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">Before I go any further I should state that I am a Librarian by training, and not a lawyer, never mind legal scholar. Nonetheless I can state four facts that demonstrate digital sovereignty is increasingly becoming an issue for countries and organizations around the world.  </p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both  Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the  privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in  their respective jurisdictions.   </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In  Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies,  particularly with respect to employees working in public service  </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Finally,  Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May,  2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in  Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August  2024 to 100 in April 2026.  </li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dp8ps\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4o4eq\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4gcng\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84um5\\\\\\">The  <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD  Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As  Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel  American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if  that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres  located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this  represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world  and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.  </li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge  Analytica data scandal  </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft  doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google  sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a6dbk\\\\\\"><b>New Alternatives, New Opportunities</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 366, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T01:20:51.642Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
407	2026-06-07 18:11:00.474442+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), had I also been subject to U.S. laws, perhaps before I even got out of bed?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic proccesses around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Without sounding hyperbolic, I'll state a simple fact that U.S. Technology companies act as an oligarchy. That is, they use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 406, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T18:10:58.525Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
430	2026-06-14 18:29:02.073336+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and splashes of coffee I spill on my shirt while I will myself awake. At my waking moments, it's not uncommon for me to ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\", as the sun and digital screens beam onto my face for the first moments of the day. As the reality of my day begins to set in,  that question of where I am turns out has less to do find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 429, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:29:01.055Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
424	2026-06-14 18:10:46.995493+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 424, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:09:55.504Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
456	2026-06-14 19:15:13.066757+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one. This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement internationally, known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question about democracy. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\"> There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 456, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T19:15:07.582Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
369	2026-06-07 04:36:41.567323+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">Hi Everyone,</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2phoc\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups, violin practice while the watering plants, but the reality is more like hitting snooze on my alarm, watching a YouTube short, and a slog to the coffee pot. If I have enough impulse control to ignore the onslaught my social media algorithm, I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading off to work. The question I keep asking myself, if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), who\\\\u2019s legal jurisdiction was I in while using their products.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">Before I go any further I should state that I am a Librarian by training, and not a lawyer, never mind a legal scholar. Nonetheless I can state four facts that demonstrate digital sovereignty is increasingly becoming an issue for countries and organisations around the world.</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Finally, Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btej\\\\\\">The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a6dbk\\\\\\"><b>New Alternatives, New Opportunities</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 369, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T04:36:14.058Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
458	2026-06-14 19:22:30.797085+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one. This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement internationally, known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question about democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for our legislators who appoint or elect judges, who then help shape the rule of law of that country. (I'd argue democracy is actually a much larger process, I.E. if you volunteered in a bake sale, joined a non-profit or rode your bike to work, that was also a democratic act, but that's for a different blog post). </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 458, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T19:22:29.757Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
388	2026-06-07 17:06:38.306434+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole consisting of YouTube short and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. The question I keep asking myself: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), who\\\\u2019s legal jurisdiction was I in while using their products?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">Before I delve further I need to clarify that I'm a librarian, and not a lawyer. Here's three facts that demonstrate digital sovereignty is increasing interest for individuals, organisations and even nations states:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d3jsi\\\\\\">There have been many red flags that have demonstrated why we need a refuge from massive American tech companies. For me personally the tipping point occurred when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. But for many, the realisation occurred much earlier with the passage of the 2018 U.S. law, The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>).</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">Going back to my morning ritual, when I wake up, I'm in Canada, which last time I checked was the democratic society in which I live. We Canadians naturally expect that we get to play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev0s8\\\\\\">But as soon as I pick my device, I'm transported to When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 388, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T17:06:34.805Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
547	2026-06-20 16:31:38.811739+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">Digital sovereignty can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, and at low cost. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aok0t\\\\\\"> Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ej82q\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 547, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T16:31:35.289Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
562	2026-06-20 19:16:38.058871+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives Including Open Source</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6j9rp\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to either offer support for open source products, or to develop their own closed-source solutions. Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 562, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:16:36.990Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
311	2026-05-24 20:48:10.839548+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States.   Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: The United States, once considered by many to be a beacon of freedom and democracy in the world, was now an authoritarian power ready to brute force its dominance against anyone it saw as ripe for exploitation. The Tech CEOs, clearly tired of virtue signalising pluralistic values were along for the ride, acting as defacto Praetorian Guard. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this moment was about to usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex, with a myriad of new innovations and solutions supported by middle powers, the open source community, and new players trying to enter the market.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7kupm\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 310, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T20:47:57.319Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
315	2026-05-24 21:02:30.198391+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived.  Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing Tarifs only hastened a trend that was already occurring. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 314, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T21:02:23.432Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
329	2026-05-25 02:13:35.756801+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. <b>Lets briefly discuss some pre-2025 things.</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du7rk\\\\\\"><b>Not a New Concern</b></p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fhsjo\\\\\\">All the way back in 2004 the City of Munich approved <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.theregister.com/software/2004/06/17/munich-embraces-the-penguin/1278077\\\\\\">a transition to Linux</a>. citing the extensive cost of Microsoft Licensing. </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cil48\\\\\\">That US legislation that basically states that the US Gov can request user information from another country as long as the company is American, even if the software is hosted internationally </li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36tfh\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo\\\\\\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1lfip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9qj9k\\\\\\"><b>France National Police Force</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"><b>Now let's discuss 2025 to present</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9stp5\\\\\\"><b>Let's make some dangerous predictions</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2t9rd\\\\\\"><b>Talk about the flavour rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u3i4\\\\\\"><b>Where you want to go next</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu\\\\\\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7k24j\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/2013/09/gendarmerie-linux/\\\\\\">https://www.wired.com/2013/09/gendarmerie-linux/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 328, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-25T02:12:40.833Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
408	2026-06-07 18:13:16.169842+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones where I lvi perhaps before I even got out of bed?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic proccesses around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\"> Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. It's the rule of a few over the many. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 408, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T18:13:14.660Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
337	2026-06-01 18:29:16.972041+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">Some thoughts</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 337, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-01T18:25:31.445Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Digital Sovereignty
489	2026-06-15 16:44:57.484402+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sk56\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"enoip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"77a1r\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8ua2d\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 488, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T16:44:12.200Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
62	2025-01-05 05:02:39.979922+00	{"pk": 4, "live": true, "path": "00010002", "slug": "welcome", "depth": 2, "intro": "<p data-block-key=\\"eric2\\">Your place for all the latest and greatest Dan Lerch news on the internet.</p>", "owner": 1, "title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious tech librarian", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 1, "url_path": "/welcome/", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "Welcome to danlerch.ca", "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious tech librarian", "content_type": 43, "live_revision": 60, "show_in_menus": true, "latest_revision": 60, "translation_key": "ca41e4fb-cd08-44bf-b885-629482ea1b91", "last_published_at": "2025-01-05T05:00:10.274Z", "first_published_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.494Z", "search_description": "Dan Lerch, British Columbia librarian", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-01-05T05:00:10.255Z"}	\N	4	1	43	1	Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious tech librarian
6	2024-12-28 00:45:30.895863+00	{"pk": 4, "live": true, "path": "00010002", "slug": "welcome-to-my-website", "depth": 2, "intro": "<p data-block-key=\\"eric2\\">Your place for all the latest and greatest Dan Lerch news on the internet.</p>", "owner": 1, "title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 1, "url_path": "/welcome-to-my-website/", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "Notathoughtleader danlerch.ca", "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler", "content_type": 43, "live_revision": 5, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 5, "translation_key": "ca41e4fb-cd08-44bf-b885-629482ea1b91", "last_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:44:28.764Z", "first_published_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.494Z", "search_description": "dan lerch, librarian, British Columbia", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2024-12-28T00:44:28.705Z"}	\N	4	1	43	1	Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler
500	2026-06-15 17:33:30.108326+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sk56\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8ua2d\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 499, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T17:33:28.824Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
523	2026-06-15 19:53:10.962861+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"775hm\\\\\\">As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 522, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T19:53:08.278Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
561	2026-06-20 19:10:48.344007+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives Including Open Source</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. One notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is switching from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a> (There are also reports they are migrating some users to Linux, but this isn't fully confirmed). Funny enough, the ICC Chief Prosector Karim Khan now uses an open source email account provided by Tuta, a German based company. There is also opportunities for private companies to either offer support for open source products, or to develop their own closed-source solutions. Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 560, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:10:37.224Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
580	2026-06-20 19:53:30.114832+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunties for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my bedroom. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but by their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalise vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being only one of them.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n9tg\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 579, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:52:06.957Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
