tion of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce. In </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5j6ts\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 467, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T19:44:00.176Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
306	2026-05-24 20:37:05.105262+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 46th President of the United States (AKA, the Orange Dude). Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: The United States, once considered by many to be a beacon of freedom and democracy in the world, was now an authoritarian power ready to brute force its dominance against anyone it saw as ripe for exploitation. The Tech CEOs, clearly tired of virtue signalising pluralistic values were along for the ride, acting as defacto Praetorian Guard. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this moment was about to usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex, with a mariad of new innovations and solutions supported by middle powers, the open source community, and new players trying to enter the market.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7kupm\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 306, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T20:37:01.650Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
475	2026-06-15 16:22:26.200369+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement  known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce. In truth, alternatives are everyone, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"k07b\\\\\\">There has been a lot written about why one type of product becomes more widely adopted than another. I'll write about this more in a later post, suffice to say, if digital sovereignty is to succeed, the alternatives need to easier to use then their competitors. When describing bread, legendary Baker Peter Reinhart once talked about what he called 'The Flavour Rule', that is, it didn't matter how much healthier one bread was over another, people would always chose the bread that tasted the best. Our digital experience will be the same: that is, it doesn't matter how much more privacy respecting one platform is over another, if the product is slower, harder to use, and doesn't have their friends and colleagues on it, they won't use it.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty Is Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 475, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T16:22:14.202Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
314	2026-05-24 21:02:23.432629+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived.  Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing Tarifs only hastened a trend that was already occurring. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 314, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T21:02:15.956Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
428	2026-06-14 18:22:17.751138+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and splashes of coffee I spill on my shirt while I will myself awake. It's not uncommon for me to ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\", as the sun (and digital screen) beam onto my face  for the first moments of the day. find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 427, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:22:09.172Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
422	2026-06-10 21:05:26.23544+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones where I live.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My=y thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 422, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-10T21:05:13.242Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
576	2026-06-20 19:51:38.338351+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunties for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before fully getting out of bed. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but by their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalise vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being only one of them</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n9tg\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 576, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:51:36.325Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
592	2026-06-20 20:02:04.149085+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a623a\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a76r2\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/story/us-vs-microsoft-supreme-court-case-data/\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States</a>: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my bedroom. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but by their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalise vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being only one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 591, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T20:02:03.414Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
88	2025-01-05 19:50:43.339769+00	{"pk": 4, "live": true, "path": "00010002", "slug": "welcome", "depth": 2, "intro": "<p data-block-key=\\"eric2\\">Your place for all the latest and greatest Dan Lerch news on the internet.</p>", "owner": 1, "title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious person librarian", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 1, "url_path": "/welcome/", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "Welcome to danlerch.ca", "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious technology focused librarian", "content_type": 43, "live_revision": 66, "show_in_menus": true, "latest_revision": 66, "translation_key": "ca41e4fb-cd08-44bf-b885-629482ea1b91", "last_published_at": "2025-01-05T05:15:09.576Z", "first_published_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.494Z", "search_description": "Dan Lerch, British Columbia librarian", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-01-05T05:15:09.555Z"}	\N	4	1	43	1	Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious person librarian
5	2024-12-28 00:44:28.705266+00	{"pk": 4, "live": true, "path": "00010002", "slug": "welcome-to-my-website", "depth": 2, "intro": "<p data-block-key=\\"eric2\\">Your place for all the latest and greatest Dan Lerch news on the internet.</p>", "owner": 1, "title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 1, "url_path": "/welcome-to-my-website/", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Welcome to My Website", "content_type": 43, "live_revision": 1, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 1, "translation_key": "ca41e4fb-cd08-44bf-b885-629482ea1b91", "last_published_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.494Z", "first_published_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.494Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.480Z"}	\N	4	1	43	1	Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler
514	2026-06-15 19:16:19.015961+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atl6n\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pb5l\\\\\\">\\\\\\"There have been numerous documented cases where U.S.-dominated technology platforms have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes globally. A primary example is the Facebook-Cambridge Analytica scandal, which utilized massive datasets to construct sophisticated psychological profiles. These profiles enabled micro-targeted, often invisible campaigns designed to manipulate voter sentiment and behavior in pivotal elections, including the Brexit referendum and political contests in Trinidad and Tobago.\\\\\\"</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections  including Brexit,  as well as countTrinidad and Tobago. </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 514, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T19:16:15.009Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
435	2026-06-14 18:42:40.294564+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>A Morning Ritual</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and splashes of coffee that often consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than perhaps], at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I ran through during the first 20 minutes of my day. To put it another way, if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or Canadian one.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 435, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:42:34.831Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
544	2026-06-18 02:35:33.155197+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">We\\\\u2019ve taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ej82q\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5isd8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"eb37s\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2klno\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fdgnj\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1qs2n\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 543, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-18T00:30:02.837Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
558	2026-06-20 17:17:21.569823+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. One notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is switching from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a> (There are also reports they are migrating some users to Linux, but this isn't fully confirmed). Funny enough, the ICC Chief Prosector Karim Khan now uses an open source email account provided by Tuta, a German based company. There is also opportunities for private companies to either offer support for open source products, or to develop their own closed-source solutions. Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 557, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T17:17:16.104Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
308	2026-05-24 20:38:28.805804+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States (AKA, the Orange Dude).  Some of the stated objectives of the new regime included Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: The United States, once considered by many to be a beacon of freedom and democracy in the world, was now an authoritarian power ready to brute force its dominance against anyone it saw as ripe for exploitation. The Tech CEOs, clearly tired of virtue signalising pluralistic values were along for the ride, acting as defacto Praetorian Guard. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this moment was about to usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex, with a mariad of new innovations and solutions supported by middle powers, the open source community, and new players trying to enter the market.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7kupm\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 308, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T20:38:27.798Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
577	2026-06-20 19:51:38.808295+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunties for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before fully getting out of bed. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but by their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalise vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being only one of them</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n9tg\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 576, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:51:38.338Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
406	2026-06-07 18:10:58.525569+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), had I also been subject to U.S. laws, perhaps before I even got out of bed?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic proccesses around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Without sounding hyperbolic, I'll state a simple fact that U.S. Technology companies act as an oligarchy. That is, they use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 406, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T18:09:48.085Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
290	2026-03-02 02:11:49.812768+00	{"pk": 11, "live": true, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">9 grams of salt</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e94p5\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\">Boiling Bath</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping</b></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\">1) Using a knife, divide the dough into 90 gram pieces and shape into round dough balls. Place each dough ball on a baking sheet and keep covered. I recommend lining the baking sheet with parchment to prevent sticking. I also use small garbage bags to cover rather that plastic wrap, but that's entirely up to you.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"88i8u\\\\\\">2) Roll each ball into a 30 cm (1 foot) sausage-like strip. I find it easier to apply pressure from the centre, working my way to the outside.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8slqk\\\\\\">3) Push the ends together and then roll on your work surface until you've formed the final bagel shape.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5ei\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> It is essential to keep any bagel you are not currently working on completely covered. The dough pieces can dry out very quickly making the dough much more difficult to work with.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"25\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagel Roll\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6631v\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"26\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Shapping the bagel into a sausage-like shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kg72\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"27\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Combing the ends of the bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fjaiv\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"28\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling the ends of the bagel to make a combined shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fle38\\\\\\">When finished shaping all 12 bagels, let rest at room temperature for approximately two hours until they gently risen. To test the proof, you can place a bagel in water to see if it floats. Admittedly, I rarely do this anymore.They should look similar to the images below.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"29\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9ibmo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"30\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"12 rolled bagel shapes on the tray\\\\\\"/><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n12f\\\\\\">Cold Fermentation</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3rvnd\\\\\\">Place your covered tray of bagels into the fridge and let cold ferment for at least 8 hours. This will dramatically improve the flavour and texture of the bagel, especially in the warmer months when dough rises much faster at room temperature.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"4lige\\\\\\">Preheat The Oven</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"69c4p\\\\\\">Preheat your oven to 500F (260C). If you have a baking stone it may take up to 45 minutes to get it to temperature.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"914v8\\\\\\">Boiling and Seasoning</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e6cnc\\\\\\">1) Pour some of your seasoning mix onto a plate.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bojnq\\\\\\">2) Bring a large pot of water to a boil with the baking soda (or baking powder), and the honey. *</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3g5be\\\\\\">*I usually choose a softer boil, not a rolling one, although I've seen many recipes that use this method. I find it easy enough to achieve the desired texture without destroying the dough. Also I recommend using a spider to hand the boiling process, but you can also use a large spoon.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4dbd3\\\\\\">3) Adding three bagels at a time boil each bagel for 2 minutes a side. When the four minutes are up, please each bagel into the season plate and cover both sides of the bagel. When finished you can place the bagels back on your baking sheet.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"31\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Boiling the bagels\\\\\\"/><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d7h2s\\\\\\"><b>Baking</b></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d9i5a\\\\\\">Place 6 bagels in the oven, then turn the heat down to 475F (245C). Bake for 6 minutes, flip the bagels over and bake for another 6 minutes. The bagels should have a nice char on them. If they don't cook on each side for an extra minute or two. When finished, place on a cooling rack and let sit for 20 minutes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"32\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Baking the bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"amcrh\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"33\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagels on the cooling rack\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"59br1\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"34\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"A finished bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n28p\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"35\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"A bagel with creamcheese\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"h7qm\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f4sk\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> If using a baking stone, I recommend cleaning off the excess seeds between batches. I use a dough scraper and and plate. This prevents burnt seeds from getting onto your 2nd batch of bagels. Also Since the over door has been open for a bit, I usually put the temperate back at 500F (260C) for 8 minutes.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7bnc5\\\\\\"><b>Serving</b></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ljih\\\\\\">Don't be stingy with your cream cheese. This is an important rule. Slather it on. I prefer scallion cream cheese but you do you. I find the Liberte brand in Canada is the best, but you can't go wrong with Philadelphia. Experiment with what you look: lox, tomato, butter.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uela\\\\\\"><b>Storing The Bagels</b></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f6475\\\\\\"><b>Short term:</b> I store bagels in a paper bag. Plastic bags seem to make the crust get soft. I have no idea why but this is my experience.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c6c1r\\\\\\"><b>Long Term:</b> You'll probably also notice that after about two days of room temperate storage the bagels start to stale. For longer-term storage I slice each bagel in half, place in paper bag. Then I place that paper bag in a zip-lock Freezer bag, and place in the freezer.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2026-01-17", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 289, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 289, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": "2026-01-17T23:53:07.493Z", "first_published_at": "2026-01-17T23:04:34.151Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T23:53:07.443Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
522	2026-06-15 19:53:08.278613+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"775hm\\\\\\">As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 522, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T19:53:04.266Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
540	2026-06-17 22:22:56.977096+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. [just say there's renewed interest, than prove it, don't need to talk about DT]With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a growing interest by government and citizens in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ehgdk\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"as0n2\\\\\\">There was also of course the revelations made by <a href=\\\\\\"https://spyscape.com/article/15-top-nsa-spy-secrets-revealed-by-snowden\\\\\\">Edward Snowden</a> where, ...</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ejvfi\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008 <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>, sought to use alternatives to Microsoft including Linux and LibreOffice. They stated \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"775hm\\\\\\">As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping. [say something about how this relates to democracy]</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 540, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-17T22:22:54.471Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
423	2026-06-10 21:05:49.714638+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones where I live.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 422, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-10T21:05:26.235Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
289	2026-01-17 23:53:07.443196+00	{"pk": 11, "live": true, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\">Boiling Bath</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping</b></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\">1) Using a knife, divide the dough into 90 gram pieces and shape into round dough balls. Place each dough ball on a baking sheet and keep covered. I recommend lining the baking sheet with parchment to prevent sticking. I also use small garbage bags to cover rather that plastic wrap, but that's entirely up to you.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"88i8u\\\\\\">2) Roll each ball into a 30 cm (1 foot) sausage-like strip. I find it easier to apply pressure from the centre, working my way to the outside.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8slqk\\\\\\">3) Push the ends together and then roll on your work surface until you've formed the final bagel shape.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5ei\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> It is essential to keep any bagel you are not currently working on completely covered. The dough pieces can dry out very quickly making the dough much more difficult to work with.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"25\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagel Roll\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6631v\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"26\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Shapping the bagel into a sausage-like shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kg72\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"27\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Combing the ends of the bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fjaiv\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"28\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling the ends of the bagel to make a combined shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fle38\\\\\\">When finished shaping all 12 bagels, let rest at room temperature for approximately two hours until they gently risen. To test the proof, you can place a bagel in water to see if it floats. Admittedly, I rarely do this anymore.They should look similar to the images below.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"29\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9ibmo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"30\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"12 rolled bagel shapes on the tray\\\\\\"/><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n12f\\\\\\">Cold Fermentation</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3rvnd\\\\\\">Place your covered tray of bagels into the fridge and let cold ferment for at least 8 hours. This will dramatically improve the flavour and texture of the bagel, especially in the warmer months when dough rises much faster at room temperature.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"4lige\\\\\\">Preheat The Oven</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"69c4p\\\\\\">Preheat your oven to 500F (260C). If you have a baking stone it may take up to 45 minutes to get it to temperature.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"914v8\\\\\\">Boiling and Seasoning</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e6cnc\\\\\\">1) Pour some of your seasoning mix onto a plate.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bojnq\\\\\\">2) Bring a large pot of water to a boil with the baking soda (or baking powder), and the honey. *</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3g5be\\\\\\">*I usually choose a softer boil, not a rolling one, although I've seen many recipes that use this method. I find it easy enough to achieve the desired texture without destroying the dough. Also I recommend using a spider to hand the boiling process, but you can also use a large spoon.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4dbd3\\\\\\">3) Adding three bagels at a time boil each bagel for 2 minutes a side. When the four minutes are up, please each bagel into the season plate and cover both sides of the bagel. When finished you can place the bagels back on your baking sheet.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"31\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Boiling the bagels\\\\\\"/><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d7h2s\\\\\\"><b>Baking</b></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d9i5a\\\\\\">Place 6 bagels in the oven, then turn the heat down to 475F (245C). Bake for 6 minutes, flip the bagels over and bake for another 6 minutes. The bagels should have a nice char on them. If they don't cook on each side for an extra minute or two. When finished, place on a cooling rack and let sit for 20 minutes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"32\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Baking the bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"amcrh\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"33\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagels on the cooling rack\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"59br1\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"34\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"A finished bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n28p\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"35\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"A bagel with creamcheese\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"h7qm\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f4sk\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> If using a baking stone, I recommend cleaning off the excess seeds between batches. I use a dough scraper and and plate. This prevents burnt seeds from getting onto your 2nd batch of bagels. Also Since the over door has been open for a bit, I usually put the temperate back at 500F (260C) for 8 minutes.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7bnc5\\\\\\"><b>Serving</b></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ljih\\\\\\">Don't be stingy with your cream cheese. This is an important rule. Slather it on. I prefer scallion cream cheese but you do you. I find the Liberte brand in Canada is the best, but you can't go wrong with Philadelphia. Experiment with what you look: lox, tomato, butter.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uela\\\\\\"><b>Storing The Bagels</b></h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f6475\\\\\\"><b>Short term:</b> I store bagels in a paper bag. Plastic bags seem to make the crust get soft. I have no idea why but this is my experience.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"c6c1r\\\\\\"><b>Long Term:</b> You'll probably also notice that after about two days of room temperate storage the bagels start to stale. For longer-term storage I slice each bagel in half, place in paper bag. Then I place that paper bag in a zip-lock Freezer bag, and place in the freezer.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2026-01-17", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": 288, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 288, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": "2026-01-17T23:51:53.276Z", "first_published_at": "2026-01-17T23:04:34.151Z", "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T23:51:53.217Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
271	2026-01-17 22:16:52.872721+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9k7b0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\">Using a knife, divide the dough into 90 gram balls. Place them on a baking sheet and keep covered. I recommend lining the baking sheet with parchment to prevent sticking. I also use small garbage bags to cover rather that plastic wrap, but that's entirely up to you.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"88i8u\\\\\\">Roll each ball into a 30 cm (1 foot) sausage-like strip. I find it easier to apply pressure from the centre, working my way to the outside. Push the ends together and then roll on your work surface until you've formed the final bagel shape.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5ei\\\\\\">It is essential to keep any bagel you are not currently working on completely covered. The dough pieces can dry out very quickly making the dough much more difficult to work with.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"25\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagel Roll\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6631v\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"26\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Shapping the bagel into a sausage-like shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kg72\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"27\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Combing the ends of the bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fjaiv\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"28\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling the ends of the bagel to make a combined shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fle38\\\\\\">When finished shaping all 12 bagels, let rest at room temperature for approximately two hours until they gently risen. To test the proof, you can place a bagel in water to see if it floats. Admittedly, I rarely do this anymore.They should look similar to the images below. </p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"29\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9ibmo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"30\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"12 rolled bagel shapes on the tray\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n12f\\\\\\"></p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 270, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T22:16:22.055Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
274	2026-01-17 22:20:57.576486+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\">1) Using a knife, divide the dough into 90 gram pieces and shape into round dough balls. Place each dough ball on a baking sheet and keep covered. I recommend lining the baking sheet with parchment to prevent sticking. I also use small garbage bags to cover rather that plastic wrap, but that's entirely up to you.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"88i8u\\\\\\">2) Roll each ball into a 30 cm (1 foot) sausage-like strip. I find it easier to apply pressure from the centre, working my way to the outside.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8slqk\\\\\\">3) Push the ends together and then roll on your work surface until you've formed the final bagel shape.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5ei\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> It is essential to keep any bagel you are not currently working on completely covered. The dough pieces can dry out very quickly making the dough much more difficult to work with.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"25\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagel Roll\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6631v\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"26\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Shapping the bagel into a sausage-like shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kg72\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"27\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Combing the ends of the bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fjaiv\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"28\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling the ends of the bagel to make a combined shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fle38\\\\\\">When finished shaping all 12 bagels, let rest at room temperature for approximately two hours until they gently risen. To test the proof, you can place a bagel in water to see if it floats. Admittedly, I rarely do this anymore.They should look similar to the images below.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"29\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9ibmo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"30\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"12 rolled bagel shapes on the tray\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n12f\\\\\\">Place your covered tray of bagels into the fridge and let cold ferment for at least 8 hours.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 273, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T22:19:18.877Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
249	2026-01-10 19:23:11.981237+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\">Everything Mix </h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"77via\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish. </p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"i05s\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3c4nb\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doq0u\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2faqq\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9nslc\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"l4u4\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3oliq\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbhlb\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cl6qe\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"32h8f\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f55ni\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 248, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T19:09:47.999Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
254	2026-01-10 19:32:32.711123+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cl6qe\\\\\\">Poolish: In a large bowl add the whole wheat flour and bread flour. Then add the water and sourdough starter.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar? </b>I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the receipe. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"hbpc\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 253, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T19:30:27.277Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
259	2026-01-10 20:05:02.285792+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9k7b0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"76i6h\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping and Waiting: </b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"604vs\\\\\\"><b>Boiling:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the receipe.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"hbpc\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 258, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T20:02:18.645Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
60	2025-01-05 05:00:10.255315+00	{"pk": 4, "live": true, "path": "00010002", "slug": "welcome", "depth": 2, "intro": "<p data-block-key=\\"eric2\\">Your place for all the latest and greatest Dan Lerch news on the internet.</p>", "owner": 1, "title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious tech librarian", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 1, "url_path": "/welcome/", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "Welcome to danlerch.ca", "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler", "content_type": 43, "live_revision": 8, "show_in_menus": true, "latest_revision": 8, "translation_key": "ca41e4fb-cd08-44bf-b885-629482ea1b91", "last_published_at": "2024-12-28T01:14:08.299Z", "first_published_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.494Z", "search_description": "dan lerch, librarian, British Columbia", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2024-12-28T01:14:08.276Z"}	\N	4	1	43	1	Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious tech librarian
244	2026-01-02 00:15:33.388673+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">30 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">40 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">375 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"i05s\\\\\\">Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3c4nb\\\\\\">Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doq0u\\\\\\">Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2faqq\\\\\\">Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9nslc\\\\\\">Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"l4u4\\\\\\">Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3oliq\\\\\\">Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cl6qe\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"32h8f\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f55ni\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 243, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-02T00:14:41.670Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
250	2026-01-10 19:24:25.08844+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cbhlb\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cl6qe\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f55ni\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 249, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T19:23:11.981Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
255	2026-01-10 19:37:09.038109+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3t377\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cl6qe\\\\\\">Poolish: In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels. </p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the receipe.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"hbpc\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 254, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T19:32:32.711Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
381	2026-06-07 05:54:48.859409+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups, violin practice while the watering plants, but the reality is more like hitting snooze on my alarm, watching a YouTube short, and a slog to the coffee pot. If I have enough impulse control to ignore the onslaught my social media algorithm, I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading off to work. The question I keep asking myself: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), who\\\\u2019s legal jurisdiction was I in while using their products?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">Before I go any further I should state that I am a Librarian by training, and not a lawyer, <i>never mind a legal scholar.</i> Nonetheless I can state three facts that demonstrate digital sovereignty is increasing interest for individuals, organisations and nations states.</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d3jsi\\\\\\">There have been many red flags that have demonstrated why we need a refuge from massive American tech companies. For me personally the tipping point occurred when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. But for many, the realisation occurred much earlier with the passage of the 2018 U.S. law, The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>).</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">Going back to my morning ritual, when I wake up, I'm in Canada, which last time I checked was the democratic society in which I live. We Canadians naturally expect that we get to play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev0s8\\\\\\">But as soon as I pick my device, I'm transported to When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 380, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T05:23:01.688Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
433	2026-06-14 18:42:00.209063+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and splashes of coffee that often consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than perhaps]at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I ran through during the first 20 minutes of my day. To put it another way, if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or Canadian one.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 432, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:41:20.475Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
316	2026-05-24 21:04:35.578377+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 316, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T21:04:26.549Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
305	2026-05-24 20:34:39.779989+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the new presidident of the United States (AKA, the Orange Dude). Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg, Elon Musk, Sundar Pichai, Jeff Bezos, and Tim Cook. While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: The United States, once considered by many to be a beacon of freedom and democracy in the world, was now an authoritarian power ready to brute force its dominance against anyone it saw as ripe for exploitation. The Tech CEOs, clearly tired of virtue signalising pluralistic values were along for the ride, acting as defacto Praetorian Guard. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this moment was about to usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex, with a mariad of new innovations and solutions supported by middle powers, the open source community, and new players trying to enter the market.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7kupm\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 304, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T20:34:36.779Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
313	2026-05-24 20:49:28.184187+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: The United States, once considered by many to be a beacon of freedom and democracy in the world, was now an authoritarian power ready to brute force its dominance against anyone it saw as ripe for exploitation. The Tech CEOs, clearly tired of virtue signalising pluralistic values were along for the ride, acting as defacto Praetorian Guard. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this moment was about to usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex, with a myriad of new innovations and solutions supported by middle powers, the open source community, and new players trying to enter the market.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7kupm\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 312, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T20:49:25.286Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
317	2026-05-24 21:04:38.612135+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 316, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-24T21:04:35.578Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
377	2026-06-07 04:54:26.851884+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups, violin practice while the watering plants, but the reality is more like hitting snooze on my alarm, watching a YouTube short, and a slog to the coffee pot. If I have enough impulse control to ignore the onslaught my social media algorithm, I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading off to work. The question I keep asking myself: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), who\\\\u2019s legal jurisdiction was I in while using their products?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">Before I go any further I should state that I am a Librarian by training, and not a lawyer, <i>never mind a legal scholar.</i> Nonetheless I can state three facts that demonstrate digital sovereignty is increasing interest for individuals, organisations and nations states.</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d3jsi\\\\\\">There have been many red flags that have demonstrated why we need a refuge from massive American tech companies. For me personally the tipping point occurred when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. But for many, the realization occured with the passing of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">In order for a society to be considered democratic, we'd naturally expect that citizens would play a key role in shaping who runs the government and what laws get passed. We'd also have the ability to access to the rule of law, and be able to express ourselves through a free press. Finally, we'd expect reasonable regulation to place boundaries on the private sector, ensuring the business community reflects the values and culture of our communities. When it comes to The Internet, the challenge has become that four or five companies in one country have become so big and omnipresent that it essentially supersedes the democratic will of the country. Here are a few examples of big tech companies misusing their influence to undermine democratic processes:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a6dbk\\\\\\"><b>New Alternatives, New Opportunities</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 377, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T04:54:23.360Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
570	2026-06-20 19:26:04.347379+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunties for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 570, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:25:54.328Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
404	2026-06-07 18:03:38.230424+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), had I also been subject to U.S. laws, perhaps before I even got out of bed?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic proccesses around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Without sounding hyperbolic, U.S. Technology companies act as a defacto oligarchy. That is, they use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 404, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T18:03:23.208Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
398	2026-06-07 17:55:06.369673+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), had I also been subject to U.S. laws, perhaps before I even got out of bed?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic proccesses around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 398, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T17:55:03.379Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
427	2026-06-14 18:22:09.17203+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and splashes of coffee I spill on my shirt while I will myself awake. It's not uncommon for me to ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\", as the sun (and digital screen) beam onto my face  for the first moments of the day. find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 427, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:22:06.668Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
518	2026-06-15 19:45:01.230058+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atl6n\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5pb5l\\\\\\"></p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S. </li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 518, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T19:44:58.723Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
257	2026-01-10 20:00:48.099281+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6db74\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ta6a\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ji5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f7828\\\\\\"></p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3t377\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cl6qe\\\\\\">Poolish: In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the receipe.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"hbpc\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 256, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T20:00:31.499Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
262	2026-01-10 20:06:57.23292+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9k7b0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"76i6h\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping and Waiting:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"604vs\\\\\\"><b>Boiling:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 261, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-10T20:06:38.077Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
265	2026-01-17 21:39:56.779228+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9k7b0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sqs0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"></p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"76i6h\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping and Waiting:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"604vs\\\\\\"><b>Boiling:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 264, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T21:34:42.101Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
270	2026-01-17 22:16:22.055354+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9k7b0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\">Using a knife, divide the dough into 90 gram balls. Place them on a baking sheet and keep covered. I recommend lining the baking sheet with parchment to prevent sticking. I also use small garbage bags to cover rather that plastic wrap, but that's entirely up to you.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"88i8u\\\\\\">Roll each ball into a 30 cm (1 foot) sausage-like strip. I find it easier to apply pressure from the centre, working my way to the outside. Push the ends together and then roll on your work surface until you've formed the final bagel shape.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5ei\\\\\\">It is essential to keep any bagel you are not currently working on completely covered. The dough pieces can dry out very quickly making the dough much more difficult to work with.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"25\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagel Roll\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6631v\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"26\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Shapping the bagel into a sausage-like shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kg72\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"27\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Combing the ends of the bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fjaiv\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"28\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling the ends of the bagel to make a combined shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fle38\\\\\\">When finished shaping all 12 bagels, let rest at room temperature for approximately two hours until they gently risen. To test the proof, you can place a bagel in water to see if it floats. Admittedly, I rarely do this anymore. </p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"29\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9ibmo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"30\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"12 rolled bagel shapes on the tray\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n12f\\\\\\"></p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 269, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T22:13:21.275Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
273	2026-01-17 22:19:18.877815+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9k7b0\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\">1) Using a knife, divide the dough into 90 gram pieces and shape into round dough balls. Place each dough ball on a baking sheet and keep covered. I recommend lining the baking sheet with parchment to prevent sticking. I also use small garbage bags to cover rather that plastic wrap, but that's entirely up to you.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"88i8u\\\\\\">2) Roll each ball into a 30 cm (1 foot) sausage-like strip. I find it easier to apply pressure from the centre, working my way to the outside. Push the ends together and then roll on your work surface until you've formed the final bagel shape.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5ei\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> It is essential to keep any bagel you are not currently working on completely covered. The dough pieces can dry out very quickly making the dough much more difficult to work with.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"25\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagel Roll\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6631v\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"26\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Shapping the bagel into a sausage-like shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kg72\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"27\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Combing the ends of the bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fjaiv\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"28\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling the ends of the bagel to make a combined shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fle38\\\\\\">When finished shaping all 12 bagels, let rest at room temperature for approximately two hours until they gently risen. To test the proof, you can place a bagel in water to see if it floats. Admittedly, I rarely do this anymore.They should look similar to the images below.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"29\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9ibmo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"30\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"12 rolled bagel shapes on the tray\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n12f\\\\\\">Place your covered tray of bagels into the fridge and let cold ferment for at least 8 hours.</p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 272, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T22:17:51.005Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
536	2026-06-16 02:51:38.730178+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a growing interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ehgdk\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"as0n2\\\\\\">There was also of course the revelations made by <a href=\\\\\\"https://spyscape.com/article/15-top-nsa-spy-secrets-revealed-by-snowden\\\\\\">Edward Snowden</a> where, ...</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ejvfi\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008 <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>, sought to use alternatives to Microsoft including Linux and LibreOffice. They stated \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"775hm\\\\\\">As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping. [say something about how this relates to democracy]</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 535, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-16T02:51:35.101Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
501	2026-06-15 18:23:16.109658+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sk56\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8ua2d\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 500, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T17:33:30.108Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
439	2026-06-14 18:46:46.75192+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>A Morning Ritual</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and gulps of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I ran through during the first 20 minutes of my day. To put it another way, if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or  have I remCanadian one.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 439, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:46:44.734Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
481	2026-06-15 16:26:09.607058+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>The Flavour Rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"k07b\\\\\\">There has been a lot written about why one type of product becomes more widely adopted than another. I'll write about this more in a later post, suffice to say, if digital sovereignty is to succeed, the alternatives need to easier to use then their competitors. When describing bread, legendary Baker Peter Reinhart once talked about what he called 'The Flavour Rule', that is, it didn't matter how much healthier one bread was over another, people would always chose the bread that tasted the best. Our digital experience will be the same: that is, it doesn't matter how much more privacy respecting one platform is over another, if the product is slower, harder to use, and doesn't have their friends and colleagues on it, they won't use it.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 481, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T16:26:06.594Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
276	2026-01-17 22:27:49.306078+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Why Bagels?</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36kbd\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. I also kneed everything by hand, although you can use a stand mixer for the final dough. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">335 grams water</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"b4doq\\\\\\">40 grams sourdough starter (recently refreshed)</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">20 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"80jrs\\\\\\">45 grams vital wheat gluten</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">380 bread flour</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\"><b>Water Bath</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ko6l\\\\\\">In a pot of water add:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5h0\\\\\\">1 tbsp bakerspoon (or 1 tsp of baking soda)</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mpn9\\\\\\">3 tbsp honey</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"5l0v3\\\\\\">Everything Mix (optional)</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6tv96\\\\\\">This is just a general guide. You can use whatever seasoning or ratios you wish.</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"6c3e9\\\\\\">4 tbsp Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3imnq\\\\\\">4 tbsp Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"btqhi\\\\\\">1 tbsp Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doflc\\\\\\">1 tbsp Onion Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"1fgqf\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fqomn\\\\\\">1/4 tbsp Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3k3h5\\\\\\">1/2 tsp Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2ijc5\\\\\\"><b>Poolish</b>:</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5esp3\\\\\\">Prep time: 10 minutes</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bh7u0\\\\\\">Total time: 4 to 8 hours</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5k0jj\\\\\\">In a large bowl mix the whole wheat flour, bread flour, water and sourdough starter. Wait between 4 and 8 hours for the poolish to become bubbly. In winter I usually do this step the evening before I make the bagels.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"20\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"sourdough starter\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fvqv5\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"22\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Another photos of unmixed poolish\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"29pam\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"21\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"poolish, all ingredients unmixed\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d03br\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"23\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Fully fermented poolish\\\\\\"/><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"bsio\\\\\\"><b>Final Dough:</b></h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3d80t\\\\\\">Add the malt powder, salt, honey, and sugar, and vital wheat glut to the poolish. Add the bread flour and mix with a wooden spoon until all the ingredients are loosely combined. Kneed by hand for approximately 5 minutes. Cover the bowl and let sit for approximately 5 minutes. Kneed again until the it becomes elastic. When stretched, you should be able to see through it. This is often called the <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/blog/2022/10/14/what-is-the-windowpane-test-for-bread-dough\\\\\\">windowpane test</a>. Let the dough rise for 2 hours.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"24\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"the window pane test\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6qibc\\\\\\"><b>Dividing, Shaping</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a1eni\\\\\\">1) Using a knife, divide the dough into 90 gram pieces and shape into round dough balls. Place each dough ball on a baking sheet and keep covered. I recommend lining the baking sheet with parchment to prevent sticking. I also use small garbage bags to cover rather that plastic wrap, but that's entirely up to you.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"88i8u\\\\\\">2) Roll each ball into a 30 cm (1 foot) sausage-like strip. I find it easier to apply pressure from the centre, working my way to the outside.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8slqk\\\\\\">3) Push the ends together and then roll on your work surface until you've formed the final bagel shape.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dh5ei\\\\\\"><b>Note:</b> It is essential to keep any bagel you are not currently working on completely covered. The dough pieces can dry out very quickly making the dough much more difficult to work with.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"25\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Bagel Roll\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6631v\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"26\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Shapping the bagel into a sausage-like shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"kg72\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"27\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Combing the ends of the bagel\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fjaiv\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"28\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling the ends of the bagel to make a combined shape\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"fle38\\\\\\">When finished shaping all 12 bagels, let rest at room temperature for approximately two hours until they gently risen. To test the proof, you can place a bagel in water to see if it floats. Admittedly, I rarely do this anymore.They should look similar to the images below.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"29\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Rolling bagels\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9ibmo\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"left\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"30\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"12 rolled bagel shapes on the tray\\\\\\"/><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n12f\\\\\\">Cold Fermentation</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3rvnd\\\\\\">Place your covered tray of bagels into the fridge and let cold ferment for at least 8 hours. This will dramatically improve the flavour and texture of the bagel, especially in the warmer months when dough rises much faster at room temperature.</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"914v8\\\\\\">Boiling</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e6cnc\\\\\\">1) Bring a large pot of water to boil with baking soda (or baking powder), and the honey. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4dbd3\\\\\\">2) I usually do a softer boil, but many recipes call for a rolling one, but I've found the softer boil is easier to manage. Using a spider or a large cooking spoon, boil the bagels for 2 minutes a side. </p><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"d12aq\\\\\\"><b>Baking:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"8i1kg\\\\\\"><b>Storing:</b></h3><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h3><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\"><b>Diastatic malt powder:</b> Depending on your location diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find. While it adds a nice sweetness to the bagel it is not strictly essential. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.weekendbakery.com/posts/making-your-own-diastatic-malt/\\\\\\">making your own</a> or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention!</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6mgcj\\\\\\"><b>Vital Wheat Gluten:</b> Part of what gives a bagel its signature chewiness is the high gluten content of the dough. Vital wheat gluten (aka bread improver) has used for centuries. It's essentially bread flour with most of its starch washed off to give a higher protein content. It can be omitted by substituting bread flour.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4kmm\\\\\\"><b>Sourdough Starter:</b> Lots has been said on sourdough starter and how to make one. Here's <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/sourdough-starter-recipe\\\\\\">one article</a> I found helpful many years ago.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"g7iu\\\\\\"><b>What's with the sugar?</b> I've found sugar helps develop the hard crust on the bagel. If you're not down with this, you can alter the recipe.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 275, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-01-17T22:22:40.044Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
548	2026-06-20 16:31:39.289419+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">Digital sovereignty can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, and at low cost. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aok0t\\\\\\"> Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ej82q\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 547, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T16:31:38.811Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
581	2026-06-20 19:53:32.388629+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fireo and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole, I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunties for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my bedroom. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but by their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalise vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being only one of them.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1n9tg\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 580, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:53:30.114Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
524	2026-06-15 19:54:40.083537+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"775hm\\\\\\">As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 524, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T19:54:37.589Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
530	2026-06-15 20:13:54.963618+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a growing interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ehgdk\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"as0n2\\\\\\">There was also of course the revelations made by <a href=\\\\\\"https://spyscape.com/article/15-top-nsa-spy-secrets-revealed-by-snowden\\\\\\">Edward Snowden</a> where, ...</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ejvfi\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008 <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>, sought to use alternatives to Microsoft including Linux and LibreOffice. They stated \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\" </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"775hm\\\\\\">As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 530, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T20:13:52.463Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
465	2026-06-14 19:42:43.278767+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one. This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement internationally, known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternative Products</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, media streaming, and E-commerce. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5j6ts\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 465, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T19:42:37.789Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
66	2025-01-05 05:15:09.555237+00	{"pk": 4, "live": true, "path": "00010002", "slug": "welcome", "depth": 2, "intro": "<p data-block-key=\\"eric2\\">Your place for all the latest and greatest Dan Lerch news on the internet.</p>", "owner": 1, "title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious technology focused librarian", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 1, "url_path": "/welcome/", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "Welcome to danlerch.ca", "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious tech librarian", "content_type": 43, "live_revision": 62, "show_in_menus": true, "latest_revision": 62, "translation_key": "ca41e4fb-cd08-44bf-b885-629482ea1b91", "last_published_at": "2025-01-05T05:02:40.000Z", "first_published_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.494Z", "search_description": "Dan Lerch, British Columbia librarian", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-01-05T05:02:39.979Z"}	\N	4	1	43	1	Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious technology focused librarian
8	2024-12-28 01:14:08.276899+00	{"pk": 4, "live": true, "path": "00010002", "slug": "welcome", "depth": 2, "intro": "<p data-block-key=\\"eric2\\">Your place for all the latest and greatest Dan Lerch news on the internet.</p>", "owner": 1, "title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 1, "url_path": "/welcome-to-my-website/", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "Welcome to danlerch.ca", "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler", "content_type": 43, "live_revision": 6, "show_in_menus": true, "latest_revision": 6, "translation_key": "ca41e4fb-cd08-44bf-b885-629482ea1b91", "last_published_at": "2024-12-28T00:45:30.919Z", "first_published_at": "2024-08-06T18:23:49.494Z", "search_description": "dan lerch, librarian, British Columbia", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": false, "latest_revision_created_at": "2024-12-28T00:45:30.895Z"}	\N	4	1	43	1	Dan Lerch: Notes from a curious data wrangler
419	2026-06-07 18:25:02.576243+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones where I live.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 418, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T18:24:46.890Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
447	2026-06-14 18:57:45.179705+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and especially in  Europe. Some recent examples include:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 446, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:57:45.014Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
