reignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives Including Open Source</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box that is Canadian-based. At present they claim to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a>. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2afc9\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5nd4f\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 564, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:21:36.187Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
442	2026-06-14 18:47:10.980406+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>A Morning Ritual</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I ran through during the first 20 minutes of my day. To put it another way, if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For many of us, a tipping point occurred in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. My thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 441, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T18:47:09.909Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
462	2026-06-14 19:26:25.532407+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one. This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement internationally, known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own invidual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 461, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T19:26:09.412Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
484	2026-06-15 16:34:11.235495+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8ua2d\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 483, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T16:34:08.253Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
341	2026-06-01 18:41:34.560555+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">Some thoughts</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 340, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-01T18:41:28.245Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
328	2026-05-25 02:12:40.833397+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">On Monday January 20, 2025 some of the largest, wealthiest, and most powerful tech billionaires attended the Inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Attendees included Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Elon Musk (X / Space X/ Tesla), Sundar Pichai (Google), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Tim Cook (Apple). While Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella may not have attended, Microsoft nonetheless donated <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.euronews.com/next/2025/01/18/donald-trumps-inauguration-which-tech-leaders-will-be-attending-and-who-will-skip-it\\\\\\">1 million to the inaugural fund</a>. The image was clear: all the liberal virtue signaling about pluralist values was gone. What these companies wanted more than anything was access to power, no matter how grotesque that power might be perceived. Mark Zuckerberg, announcing the dissolution of the Trust and Safety Teams at Facebook all but confirmed this sentiment stating: I think we're just in a different moment. One can be forgiven for thinking this would usher in a new dark age of US dominated technology, but the truth may turn out to be far more complex.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dqk1r\\\\\\">For years now, governments across the world have become increasingly concerned with US dominated technology, and the legal apparatus that ties citizens of another country to the United States. And for many years, there have been a variety of new players trying to enter the market. The new US administration, with it talks of making Canada the 51st State, withdrawing from NATO, or implementing tarifs only hastened that trend. <b>Lets briefly discuss some pre-2025 things.</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du7rk\\\\\\"><b>Not a New Concern</b></p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"fhsjo\\\\\\">All the way back in 2004 the City of Munich approved <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.theregister.com/software/2004/06/17/munich-embraces-the-penguin/1278077\\\\\\">a transition to Linux</a>. citing the extensive cost of Microsoft Licensing. </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cil48\\\\\\">That US legislation that basically states that the US Gov can request user information from another country as long as the company is American, even if the software is hosted internationally </li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"36tfh\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo\\\\\\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s4hoEjRh1Eo</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"1lfip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9qj9k\\\\\\"><b>France National Police Force</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d08uq\\\\\\"><b>Now let's discuss 2025 to present</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9stp5\\\\\\"><b>Let's make some dangerous predictions</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2t9rd\\\\\\"><b>Talk about the flavour rule</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"u3i4\\\\\\"><b>Where you want to go next</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e4ffs\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu\\\\\\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7k24j\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.wired.com/2013/09/gendarmerie-linux/\\\\\\">https://www.wired.com/2013/09/gendarmerie-linux/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5rl7i\\\\\\"></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/some-thoughts-on-digital-sovereignty/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 328, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-05-25T02:12:30.837Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Some Thoughts on Digital Sovereignty
502	2026-06-15 18:24:48.869513+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sk56\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 501, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T18:23:16.109Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
525	2026-06-15 19:54:44.754056+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"775hm\\\\\\">As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 524, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T19:54:40.083Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
239	2025-12-31 21:41:01.703642+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. Enjoy!</p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\">Recipe</h2><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"c78ja\\\\\\">Poolish</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4moo3\\\\\\">150 grams of whole wheat flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"cm87u\\\\\\">50 grams of bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2n5h4\\\\\\">365 grams water</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">Final Dough</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"4biv0\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"qmj8\\\\\\">30 grams sourdough starter</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">30 grams sugar</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"esh42\\\\\\">30 grams honey</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"7a39j\\\\\\">200 whole wheat</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">430 bread flour</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"68il9\\\\\\">30 grams vital wheat gluten</li></ul><h3 data-block-key=\\\\\\"1btlj\\\\\\">Everything Mix</h3><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"i05s\\\\\\">Poppy Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3c4nb\\\\\\">Sesame Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"doq0u\\\\\\">Golden Flax Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2faqq\\\\\\">Onion Flake </li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"9nslc\\\\\\">Garlic Flake</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"l4u4\\\\\\">Caraway Seeds</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3oliq\\\\\\">Course Sea Salt (or Malten Salt)</li></ul><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"7p9b3\\\\\\">Directions</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cl6qe\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"32h8f\\\\\\"></p><h2 data-block-key=\\\\\\"88rqm\\\\\\">Notes</h2><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"b7e2i\\\\\\">Diastatic malt powder can be tricky to find in some places, but adds a nice sweetness to the bagel. I've <a href=\\\\\\"https://bulkmart.ca/products/texturestar-diastatic-malt-powder-malted-milk-1-kg\\\\\\">I've linked to one site that sells it</a>, or check out the Evil Empire which I will not name. If you can't find it online in your location, you can either try making your own or skip it entirely. <i>Remember, necessity is the mother of invention! </i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aqog9\\\\\\"> </p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 238, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-12-31T21:30:32.412Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
224	2025-12-31 20:46:59.053788+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was fresh bagels. I grew up in Long Island, New York, where most towns had at least one bagel shop. Since then </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"44vj7\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qmj8\\\\\\">30 grams sourdough starter</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">30 grams sugar</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7a39j\\\\\\">200 whole wheat</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">430 bread flour</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"68il9\\\\\\">30 grams vital wheat gluten</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6bcde\\\\\\">365 grams water</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 223, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-12-29T23:16:42.743Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
235	2025-12-31 21:24:50.73673+00	{"pk": 11, "live": false, "path": "000100020003", "slug": "sourdough-bagels", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Sourdough Bagels", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bcy16\\\\\\">Ten years ago I moved to the west coast of Canada. While I was thoroughly enjoying my new life with larger mountains and more temperate weather, I couldn't quite shake that feeling like something essential was missing. That something was delicious bagels, which I frankly find hard to live without. Most places I had lived either had one decent bagel shop, or access to frozen bagels that had been shipped up from larger cities like Toronto (yes, Toronto has many great bagel shops). While trying to satisfy this craving, I had also started experimenting with sourdough bread making. Through my research I had discovered that cold fermentation often contributes to a bagel's unique favor and texture. I found that using sourdough starter was another great way to control the acidity of the dough. If you don't have a sourdough on hand you can substitute it with 1/2 teaspoon of active dry yeast. </p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"78gi1\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"cj5pj\\\\\\">30 grams diastatic malt powder</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qmj8\\\\\\">30 grams sourdough starter</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"26fha\\\\\\">30 grams sugar</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7a39j\\\\\\">200 whole wheat</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"du9r5\\\\\\">430 bread flour</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"68il9\\\\\\">30 grams vital wheat gluten</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6bcde\\\\\\">365 grams water</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2ebae49a-9a92-48ef-95aa-f4ab32e0839a\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/sourdough-bagels/", "blog_date": "2025-12-29", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 19, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Sourdough Bagels", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 234, "translation_key": "184383ef-e889-4493-80ee-0112d7ae5dfc", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2025-12-31T21:18:09.485Z"}	\N	11	1	44	1	Sourdough Bagels
509	2026-06-15 18:59:14.914486+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 509, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T18:59:08.406Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
589	2026-06-20 20:00:05.539979+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a623a\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a76r2\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my bedroom. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but by their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalise vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being only one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 589, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T19:59:57.479Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
490	2026-06-15 16:45:00.286994+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally as evidenced by:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7sk56\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"enoip\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"77a1r\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8rpne\\\\\\"></p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8ua2d\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 489, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T16:44:57.484Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
510	2026-06-15 18:59:15.187021+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a strong interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4a4or\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 509, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T18:59:14.914Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
533	2026-06-15 21:32:45.757346+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening all the time, so there will be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just fell through. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another one, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are a few examples that demonstrate a growing interest in this topic:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta and British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The Government of Canada has <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8te3n\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ehgdk\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"as0n2\\\\\\">There was also of course the revelations made by <a href=\\\\\\"https://spyscape.com/article/15-top-nsa-spy-secrets-revealed-by-snowden\\\\\\">Edward Snowden</a> where, ...</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ejvfi\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008 <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>, sought to use alternatives to Microsoft including Linux and LibreOffice. They stated \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is really a question of democracy. As citizens of a country we have both the right and responsibility to participate in a free society. As citizens we vote for members of the public to represent us, interact with the legal system, and each in our own individual way, help shape the culture of the places where we live. As the internet has become a key public utility, it goes to reason - that all things being equal - citizens should have the right to shape their own internet, and be under that particular jurisdiction. Of course, as we know, things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6u3b2\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases where U.S. dominated technology companies have been leveraged to subvert democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"775hm\\\\\\">As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"15mmt\\\\\\">In general, we've taken it as an article of faith that we're stuck with a few U.S. dominated products to deliver digital services ranging from from social media, artificial intelligence, operating systems, identity management, storage, media streaming, communications, and E-commerce, and much more. In truth, alternatives are everywhere, and in some cases widely utilised. For example, open source products such as Linux, LibreOffice, NextCloud, and Mastadon. There are also many smaller competitors gaining in the marketplace such as Proton, SyncThing, OnlyOffice, and yes even BlueSky. Ironically enough, Karim Khan now uses an email account provided by Tuta, a Gmail provider.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7qeho\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Won't Be Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4nm80\\\\\\">Democracy is two wolves and a lamb voting on what's for breakfast. Sometimes on our governments pass discriminatory laws designed to further marginalise vulnerable people. My own view is that in many situations, digital sovereignty is a tool to diversify our technology landscape, and in some instances protect citizens, but it is not a fail safe. Some other entrepreneurial librarian needs investigate this further.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dn20q\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do\\\\\\">https://decisions.scc-csc.ca/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/20302/index.do</a><br/></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 532, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-15T20:14:11.725Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
590	2026-06-20 20:00:06.103957+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. New developments, especially in Europe are happening all the time, so there should be a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"334p9\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals and Digital Rabbit Holes</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6ppl5\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I\\\\\\"? As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of the day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it seems \\\\u2013 especially when I consider the digital rabbit hole I just crawled out of. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I also been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one? This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, by simply opening their smart phone or turning on their computer. In the last few years there has been renewed interest in the topic of digital sovereignty by public officials and the courts. Here are a few notable examples:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"djbsp\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.msn.com/en-us/money/other/european-cloud-providers-call-for-digital-sovereignty-and-less-reliance-on-us-companies/ar-AA24zwfC\\\\\\">enacting digital oversight strategies</a>, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"at0n8\\\\\\">Between 2020, and 2021 The Office of The Ontario Privacy Commissioner in collaboration with the Privacy commissioners in Alberta, British Columbia, and Quebec (Commission d\\\\u2019acc\\\\u00e8s \\\\u00e0 l\\\\u2019information du Qu\\\\u00e9bec), collectively known as The Offices, investigated Clearview AI, a U.S.-based company that scraped billions of photos from social media platforms,<i> including from children</i> to build a facial recognition database. \\\\\\"<a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/opc-actions-and-decisions/investigations/investigations-into-businesses/2021/pipeda-2021-001/#toc1\\\\\\">[T]the Offices determined</a> that Clearview collected, used and disclosed the personal information of individuals in Canada for inappropriate purposes, which cannot be rendered appropriate via consent.\\\\\\"</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"c5tmt\\\\\\">The Government of Canada <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/cloud-services/digital-sovereignty/digital-sovereignty-framework-improve-digital-readiness.html\\\\\\">published a Framework</a> on how it can \\\\\\"manage and protect its data, systems and infrastructure to operate securely and independently in a globally connected environment\\\\\\"</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7nrqo\\\\\\">The public is also taking notice. Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a623a\\\\\\"></p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"a76r2\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5p1do\\\\\\"><b>Not A New Concern</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3ur4g\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in digital sovereignty, the concerns are by no means new. In 2018 The United States passed The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>). As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"75qmc\\\\\\">Even as early as 2008, The City of Munich argued that its reasons for migrating from Microsoft products to Linux (known as <a href=\\\\\\"https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/open-source-observatory-osor/document/limux-project-munich\\\\\\">The LiMux project</a>), had more to do with concerns surrounding sovereignty than cost. As They put it: \\\\\\"Rather than lowering IT costs, the main motive is the desire for strategic independence from software suppliers.\\\\\\"</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ev5pc\\\\\\"><b>Why It Matters</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"70vnu\\\\\\">The question of digital sovereignty is, at its core, a question about democracy. As citizens, we hold both the right and the responsibility to participate in a free society. We elect representatives, engage with the legal system, and\\\\u2014each in our own way\\\\u2014help shape the culture of the places we call home.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9lu3b\\\\\\">As the internet has become a fundamental public utility, it stands to reason that\\\\u2014at least in principle\\\\u2014citizens should have a voice in shaping the digital spaces where they live. But of course, as we know, all things are not equal.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"dhtee\\\\\\">There have been many documented cases in which U.S.-based technology companies were used to undermine democratic processes around the world. A few notable examples include:</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"2uoa8\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook%E2%80%93Cambridge_Analytica_data_scandal\\\\\\">Facebook-Cambridge Analytica data scandal</a> which created sophisticated psychological profiles which then Micro targeted voters to influence elections including Brexit, as well as counties such as Trinidad and Tobago</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"3n0kn\\\\\\">In May of 2025 Microsoft shut down the accounts of Karim Khan, the International Criminal Court \\\\u2019s Chief Prosecutor after being sanctioned by the U.S.</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"aorbm\\\\\\">Concern for the future digital landscape is only increasing: As Shir Weinbrand argues in an article in <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/telematics-and-informatics\\\\\\">Telematics and Informatics</a>: Google\\\\u2019s AI Overviews (AIOs) \\\\u2212 AI-generated summaries \\\\u2212 mark a shift from information retrieval to AI-driven curation, raising underexplored questions about political and news exposure and algorithmic gatekeeping.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"flo3v\\\\\\"><b>New Opportunities for Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ap8f\\\\\\">A digital sovereignty strategy can't happen without viable alternatives to U.S. dominated technology products. Open source projects are particularly well-situated to fill in the gaps, as these technologies can be deployed almost anywhere, at scale, and at low cost. This is why we've seen many governments in the E.U. begin to switch to platforms such as Linux, LibreOffice, and NextCloud. A notable example is the Danish Ministry of Digital Affairs, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/denmarks-strategic-leap-replacing-microsoft-office-365-libreoffice-digital-independence\\\\\\">which is transitioning from Microsoft Office to LibreOffice</a>; reports also indicate a move toward Linux, though this remains unconfirmed. In a similar vein, ICC Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan has adopted Tuta, a German-based provider of open-source, encrypted email services.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7poaj\\\\\\">There is also opportunities for private companies to to develop their own closed-source solutions. In Canada, the company <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/\\\\\\">Sync</a> offers cloud storage solutions similar to drop box. As of this writing they boast to have <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.sync.com/about/\\\\\\">over 7 million users</a> with global privacy compliance.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ocb\\\\\\">Nonetheless, one of the biggest challenges to a wider adoption of open source technologies relates to user experience, usability, and even accessibility. People simply won't choose to use an alternative product on the basis that it's more privacy respecting. They simply make choices on what's the most intuitive and helps them get their work done the fastest. I will have a lot more to say about this in a future post, but I'll put this analogy out there for you to consider: The last time you went to a restaurant, did you order fries, or a salad. Most likely you knew the salad was healthier, but you chose the fries. Fries just taste better.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5fk6l\\\\\\"><b>Why Digital Sovereignty Isn't Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"6fb3i\\\\\\">I began this post by describing my daily routine, with the potential ramifications of entering another country's jurisdiction before leaving my bedroom. It's important to remember that people all over the world face threats against their freedom not from a foreign country, but by their own government. To rephrase my earlier question in a new context: If I wake up in the morning, pick up my phone, and start scrolling through a sovereign social media - perhaps its called InstaCan - and my entire search history is tracked by The Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), am I more free? As we know, internet technology is often weaponized to further marginalise vulnerable populations all over the world. It's important to remember that the word democracy should be a verb, not a noun. It's a daily practice that requires many tools, with digital sovereignty perhaps being only one of them.</p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 589, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-20T20:00:05.539Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
420	2026-06-07 18:25:50.269462+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>My Morning Ritual, A Digital Adventure</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings start off with push ups and guitar practice, but the truth is I often spend it heading down a digital rabbit hole of YouTube shorts and Instagram reels. If I have enough impulse control I\\\\u2019ll stream French 24 on YouTube before heading to work. While I live and work in Canada, I find myself asking the question: if every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been subject to U.S. laws, or Canadian ones where I live.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bd21d\\\\\\">As The United States has increasingly becoming an unreliable partner (cuts to USAID, Canada the 51st State, global tariffs), there's been renewed interest in digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product. Here's are a few examples that demonstrate the renewed interest in digital sovereignty here in Canada and in Europe:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">For me personally, a tipping point occurerd in my thinking when Elon Musk (X), Mark Zuckerberg (Meta), Jeff Bezos (Amazon), and Sundar Pichai (Google) attended the inauguration of the 47th President of the United States. Suddenly my thinking went from \\\\\\"wouldn't it be nice if ...\\\\\\" to \\\\\\"we need alternatives now\\\\\\". But the truth is there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy </i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 420, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-07T18:25:45.769Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
448	2026-06-14 19:06:23.332964+00	{"pk": 12, "live": false, "path": "000100020004", "slug": "why-digital-sovereignty-matters", "depth": 3, "owner": 1, "title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "locale": 1, "locked": false, "content": "[{\\"type\\": \\"paragraph\\", \\"value\\": \\"<p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8w77o\\\\\\">This is my first entry in what I hope will be an ongoing discussion of digital sovereignty. It seems like new developments, especially out of the European Union are happening almost daily, so there\\\\u2019s a lot to talk about.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"ch5po\\\\\\"><b>Morning Rituals</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"9kv1b\\\\\\">I\\\\u2019d love to tell you that most of my mornings here in Canada begin with a routine of push ups, guitar practice, and deep meditation, rather than the flurry of YouTube shorts, Instagram reels, and sips of coffee that usually consecrate my morning. Sometimes during my waking moments, I'll ask myself, \\\\\\"wait, where am I?\\\\\\". As the synapses in my mind start to fire and the reality of day starts to come into sharper focus, that question turns out to be less trivial than it might seem, at least when I consider the digital rabbit hole I fell through during the first 20 minutes of my morning. If every moment of my morning ritual was provided by a U.S. technology company (Apple, Google, Meta), have I been travelling to a U.S. jurisdiction, or have I remained in a Canadian one. This dilemma is at the heart of a growing movement internationally, known as digital sovereignty. Digital sovereignty means that citizens residing in one country should not be subject to the laws of another country, simply by choosing to use a technology product.  With the election of the election of the 47th President of the United States there has been renewed interest in digital sovereignty both here in Canada and internationally. Here are some recent 0examples of this push include:</p><ol><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"849ge\\\\\\">Both Canadian and European Courts are making substantive rulings on the privacy rights of Citizens, even if that product is not based in their respective jurisdictions.</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"10fl9\\\\\\">In Europe, many governments are enacting digital oversight strategies, particularly with respect to employees working in the public service</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"84joi\\\\\\">The government of Canada now has a white paper on digital sovereignty with ....</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"e0v1v\\\\\\">Looking at Google Trends from the last two years (May, 2024 to May, 2026) shows a substantial increase in interest, (measured in Relative Search Volume RSV) with its lowest rating of five in August 2024 to 100 in April 2026.</li></ol><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8fp8t\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"2em0\\\\\\">While there's been growing interest in there's been growing concern about the digital sovereignty of nations, and relying too much on U.S. technology for a long time. While there are many examples the clearest and more existential was the 2018 passage of The <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.justice.gov/criminal/cloud-act-resources\\\\\\">CLOUD Act</a> (<i>Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act</i>) in the United States.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"atsc4\\\\\\">As Wired Magazine States: It allows U.S. law enforcement to compel American companies to provide access to data stored abroad, even if that data belongs to non-U.S. persons and resides in data centres located in the European Union. In the modern knowledge economy this represents a threat to sovereignty for countries across the world and certainly a threat to the democratic processes.</p><embed embedtype=\\\\\\"image\\\\\\" format=\\\\\\"fullwidth\\\\\\" id=\\\\\\"36\\\\\\" alt=\\\\\\"Digital Sovereighty Graph (world wide)\\\\\\"/><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"8dgod\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"qp6t\\\\\\"><b>Digital Sovereignty and Democracy</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"e9e0h\\\\\\">There have been many reported stories about how U.S. dominated technology companies have interfered in democratic processes around the world. Here are a few notable examples</p><ul><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"23i\\\\\\">Cambridge Analytica data scandal</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"bicom\\\\\\">Microsoft doing some shit</li><li data-block-key=\\\\\\"22f4b\\\\\\">Google sucking</li></ul><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"955os\\\\\\"><b>Alternatives</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"f0lfv\\\\\\">Technology companies act as an oligarchy. They use their combined influence to dominate the technology marketplace and limit the ability of alternatives to survive. Our recent history littered with EU fines, and American Anti-Trust. Plus let's be real, do you know anyone with an office job that isn't using Microsoft 365?</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"64g9a\\\\\\">It's the rule of a few over the many. But as Aristotle pointed out, <a href=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HMguSl8PHS4&amp;t=148s\\\\\\">oligarchy is tremendously unstable</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"19po3\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7co0j\\\\\\"><i>Make the link between DS and oligarchy</i></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"bichv\\\\\\"></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"7jp8i\\\\\\"><b>Why It\\\\u2019s Not Enough</b></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5cr74\\\\\\">References</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"27ae6\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html\\\\\\">https://www.canlii.org/en/bc/bcca/doc/2026/2026bcca67/2026bcca67.html</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"5f93t\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/\\\\\\">https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/</a></p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"3r4ae\\\\\\">Further Reading</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"d5p01\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province\\\\\\">https://www.fieldlaw.com/insights/publication/when-do-canadian-privacy-laws-apply-to-u.s.-companies#:~:text=The%20BC%20Court%20of%20Appeal%20reaffirmed%20the%20quasi%2Dconstitutional%20status,online%20activities%20and%20the%20province</a>.</p><p data-block-key=\\\\\\"4v9st\\\\\\"><a href=\\\\\\"https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look\\\\\\">https://www.hks.harvard.edu/faculty-research/policy-topics/science-technology-data/what-does-tech-oligarchy-look</a></p>\\", \\"id\\": \\"2b329050-bb7e-4fb8-965a-5377bf57640f\\"}]", "expired": false, "alias_of": null, "numchild": 0, "url_path": "/welcome/why-digital-sovereignty-matters/", "blog_date": "2026-05-24", "expire_at": null, "locked_at": null, "locked_by": null, "seo_title": "", "blog_image": 2, "go_live_at": null, "draft_title": "Why Digital Sovereignty Matters", "content_type": 44, "live_revision": null, "show_in_menus": false, "latest_revision": 448, "translation_key": "cabbe267-3396-4dfe-90c3-0156b416144b", "last_published_at": null, "first_published_at": null, "search_description": "", "wagtail_admin_comments": [], "has_unpublished_changes": true, "latest_revision_created_at": "2026-06-14T19:06:21.336Z"}	\N	12	1	44	1	Why Digital Sovereignty Matters
